"use strict";(self.webpackChunkorion_belt_docs=self.webpackChunkorion_belt_docs||[]).push([["36607"],{77735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"orion-belt-v1-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-18-orion-belt-v1-5.md","title":"Orion Belt v1.5 \u2014 SDKs, agent remote management and an AGPL license","description":"Orion Belt 1.5.0 adds Python and TypeScript SDKs for the whole API, remote management for agents, bulk grants, tunnels that survive network drops, standard API errors and the fixes from a security audit. It is now open source under the AGPL v3.0, with verifiable build provenance.","date":"2026-11-18T14:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":3.13,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-v1-5","title":"Orion Belt v1.5 \u2014 SDKs, agent remote management and an AGPL license","description":"Orion Belt 1.5.0 adds Python and TypeScript SDKs for the whole API, remote management for agents, bulk grants, tunnels that survive network drops, standard API errors and the fixes from a security audit. It is now open source under the AGPL v3.0, with verifiable build provenance.","date":"2026-11-18T14:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","self-hosted","security"],"keywords":["Orion Belt 1.5","PAM SDK","Python SDK","TypeScript SDK","SSH agent management","AGPL PAM","SLSA provenance"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Orion Belt is now open source under the AGPL v3.0","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-agpl"}},"content":"**Orion Belt 1.5.0** makes the gateway easier to automate and easier to\\ntrust. It ships SDKs for every API endpoint, lets you manage agents from the\\ngateway, and fixes everything a security audit found. It is also the first\\nrelease under an open source license, and the first with build provenance\\nyou can check.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Python and TypeScript SDKs\\n\\n`pip install orion-belt-sdk` and `npm install @orion-belt/sdk` give you a\\nclient for every `/api/v1` endpoint, with no runtime dependencies. CI checks\\nboth against the server\'s route table, so a new endpoint can\'t ship without\\nthem. See [SDKs](/docs/api/sdks) and\\n[the SDK post](/blog/orion-belt-sdks).\\n\\n## Agent remote management\\n\\n`oadmin agents reload`, `drain`, `undrain` and `update` run over the\\nconnection the agent already holds open to the gateway. No SSH to the host,\\nno inbound port. Each command is admin or operator only and audited. See\\n[Agent remote management](/docs/guides/agent-management).\\n\\n## Bulk grants and effective access\\n\\nOne call grants or revokes an access type for every user \xd7 machine pair, up\\nto 500, each applied and audited on its own. The console gains multi-select,\\nan effective access view and server-side search. See\\n[Permissions](/docs/guides/permissions-management#4-grant-or-revoke-many-at-once).\\n\\n## Tunnels that survive network drops\\n\\nAgents redial as soon as a link drops, with jittered backoff. The gateway\\npings agents and clients every 15 seconds, so dead links are noticed quickly,\\nand a reconnecting agent is no longer shown as offline.\\n\\n## Standard API errors\\n\\nEvery error now has a `code`: `invalid`, `unauthenticated`, `forbidden`,\\n`not_found`, `conflict`, `unavailable` or `internal`, each with a fixed HTTP\\nstatus. Internal details never reach a 500 response. See\\n[API errors](/docs/api#errors).\\n\\n## Security audit fixes\\n\\nAn external audit in early November found four issues; all are fixed, along\\nwith what we found while fixing them:\\n\\n- Generated agent install scripts and `install-server.sh` verify every\\n  download against the signed `checksums.txt`.\\n- Deleting a machine keeps its recordings until the retention sweep. Agent\\n  registration, credential changes and file browser actions are audited. MFA\\n  backup codes can no longer be reused.\\n- The API server has timeouts, a request body limit and security headers.\\n- With a Host CA configured, agents no longer trust a gateway that omits its\\n  certificate.\\n- The server container runs as `orionbelt`, not root.\\n\\n## Now AGPL v3.0\\n\\nOrion Belt is now open source under the GNU AGPL v3.0, replacing Apache 2.0\\nwith the Commons Clause. Using, modifying and running it stays free,\\nincluding commercially. [What changes and why](/blog/orion-belt-agpl).\\n\\n## Verifiable releases\\n\\nEvery release file and container image comes with SLSA level 3 provenance\\nand a GitHub attestation, alongside the GPG signatures. You can check that a\\nbinary was built by the release workflow from the `v1.5.0` tag. See\\n[Verify a release](/docs/quickstart/verify-release) and\\n[the post on how we build releases](/blog/verifiable-releases).\\n\\n## Upgrade notes\\n\\n- **JWT secret:** the server refuses to start with the example placeholder\\n  secret or one shorter than 32 bytes. Package installs and upgrades replace\\n  the placeholder with a random secret, which signs everyone out once. For\\n  Docker or hand-written configs, set one with `openssl rand -hex 32`.\\n- **Database migration:** the first start converts timestamp columns to\\n  `TIMESTAMPTZ`, rewriting each table once under a lock. Expect a pause on a\\n  large `audit_logs`. A non-UTC host whose time zone can\'t be detected needs\\n  `ORION_DB_LEGACY_TIMEZONE`.\\n- **Unsigned builds:** generated install scripts need signed release\\n  packages. For a local `dist/`, tick \\"Allow an unsigned local build\\".\\n- **Alpine:** the `chown` step from the 1.4.0 install guide is no longer\\n  needed; the package owns its directories correctly.\\n\\nFull notes are on the\\n[v1.5.0 release](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.5.0)."},{"id":"orion-belt-agpl","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-agpl","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-18-orion-belt-agpl.md","title":"Orion Belt is now open source under the AGPL v3.0","description":"From 1.5.0, Orion Belt is licensed under the GNU AGPL v3.0 only, replacing Apache 2.0 with the Commons Clause. What you can do, what you must share, and what it means for the SDKs.","date":"2026-11-18T13:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"}],"readingTime":2.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-agpl","title":"Orion Belt is now open source under the AGPL v3.0","description":"From 1.5.0, Orion Belt is licensed under the GNU AGPL v3.0 only, replacing Apache 2.0 with the Commons Clause. What you can do, what you must share, and what it means for the SDKs.","date":"2026-11-18T13:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["self-hosted","pam"],"keywords":["AGPL PAM","open source PAM","AGPL SSH bastion","Orion Belt license","Commons Clause to AGPL"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt v1.5 \u2014 SDKs, agent remote management and an AGPL license","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to check that an Orion Belt binary is the one we built","permalink":"/blog/verifiable-releases"}},"content":"From **1.5.0**, Orion Belt is licensed under the\\n[GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 only](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/blob/master/LICENSE)\\n(`AGPL-3.0-only`). It replaces Apache 2.0 with the Commons Clause, which we\\nused from the first release. Orion Belt is now open source in the\\n[OSI sense](https://opensource.org/license/agpl-v3), not only\\nsource-available.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What you can do\\n\\n- Run Orion Belt for any purpose, including inside a company and for paying\\n  customers\' infrastructure.\\n- Read, audit and modify the code.\\n- Package it, mirror it and redistribute it.\\n\\nThere is no fee and no \\"internal use only\\" condition. The Commons Clause\\nrestriction on selling Orion Belt is gone.\\n\\n## What you must do in return\\n\\nThe AGPL asks for one thing: if you pass the software on, you pass on the\\nsource too.\\n\\n- **Distributing it** (a modified binary, an appliance, an image you hand to\\n  others): ship the corresponding source under the AGPL.\\n- **Offering a modified version over a network** (users connect to your\\n  changed gateway): offer those users the source of your version.\\n\\nRunning an unmodified Orion Belt triggers neither. Your configuration, your\\nscripts and the machines behind the gateway are not covered by the license.\\nThis is a summary, not legal advice; the LICENSE text is what applies.\\n\\n## Why we changed\\n\\nWhen we [explained the Commons Clause](/blog/commons-clause-honesty), we\\nnamed three goals: keep the code auditable, keep self-hosting free, and stop\\na vendor from wrapping the project as a closed hosted service. The AGPL meets\\nall three. Anyone can run a hosted Orion Belt, but they have to publish the\\nchanges they make to it, which is the protection we were after.\\n\\nWhat the Commons Clause added on top was a ban on selling, and that came at a\\ncost. It is not an OSI license, so teams with an \\"OSI-approved only\\" policy\\ncould not adopt Orion Belt at all, and packagers and security reviewers had to\\nread a custom clause before they could even evaluate it. The AGPL is a license\\nthose teams already have a policy for.\\n\\n## The SDKs\\n\\nThe Python and TypeScript SDKs are AGPL too. An application that bundles an\\nSDK and is distributed, or served to others over a network, should be under an\\nAGPL-compatible license. Calling the REST API directly, with any HTTP client,\\ndoes not bring the AGPL into your code.\\n\\nThe SDK packages on PyPI and npm still carry the old license metadata from\\ntheir 1.0.0 release. Their next release picks up the AGPL.\\n\\n## Older releases\\n\\nReleases up to and including 1.4.x stay under the license they shipped with.\\nA license doesn\'t change retroactively. Upgrade to 1.5.0 to run under the\\nAGPL.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Orion Belt v1.5](/blog/orion-belt-v1-5)\\n- [Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause, before 1.5.0](/blog/commons-clause-honesty)\\n- [Open-source PAM landscape](/blog/open-source-pam-landscape)"},{"id":"verifiable-releases","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/verifiable-releases","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-18-verifiable-releases.md","title":"How to check that an Orion Belt binary is the one we built","description":"From 1.5.0, every Orion Belt release file and container image has SLSA level 3 provenance and a GitHub attestation next to its GPG signature. What each proof tells you, how to check it, and the rest of the supply-chain work behind it.","date":"2026-11-18T12:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":2.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"verifiable-releases","title":"How to check that an Orion Belt binary is the one we built","description":"From 1.5.0, every Orion Belt release file and container image has SLSA level 3 provenance and a GitHub attestation next to its GPG signature. What each proof tells you, how to check it, and the rest of the supply-chain work behind it.","date":"2026-11-18T12:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["security","ops"],"keywords":["SLSA provenance","signed releases","gh attestation verify","slsa-verifier","OpenSSF Scorecard","supply chain security PAM"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt is now open source under the AGPL v3.0","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-agpl"},"nextItem":{"title":"Automating Orion Belt from Python and TypeScript","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-sdks"}},"content":"An access gateway holds the keys to every machine behind it, so the binary\\nyou run matters as much as the code you read. From **1.5.0**, each Orion\\nBelt release lets you check not only who signed a file, but how and from\\nwhat it was built.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Three proofs, three questions\\n\\n| Proof | Question it answers |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| GPG signature (`<file>.sig`, `checksums.txt.sig`) | Was this file signed with the Orion Belt release key? |\\n| SLSA provenance (`orion-belt-v1.5.0.intoto.jsonl`) | Was it built by our release workflow, from the `v1.5.0` tag of our repository? |\\n| GitHub attestation | The same, recorded by GitHub, for release files and both container images |\\n\\nThe GPG key signs every release file and the package repositories. It\\nproves a file came from someone with the key. It can\'t tell you what was\\nbuilt: a signed binary built from a modified tree would pass.\\n\\nProvenance closes that gap. The release workflow records which repository,\\ncommit and workflow produced each file, and that statement is signed with a\\nshort-lived certificate issued to the workflow run itself. No maintainer\\nholds a key that could sign a false one.\\n\\n## Checking a file\\n\\n```bash\\nTAG=v1.5.0\\nFILE=orion-belt_1.5.0_linux_amd64.tar.gz\\ngh release download \\"$TAG\\" -R orion-belt-dev/orion-belt \\\\\\n  -p \\"$FILE\\" -p \\"orion-belt-$TAG.intoto.jsonl\\"\\n\\nslsa-verifier verify-artifact \\"$FILE\\" \\\\\\n  --provenance-path \\"orion-belt-$TAG.intoto.jsonl\\" \\\\\\n  --source-uri github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt \\\\\\n  --source-tag \\"$TAG\\"\\n\\ngh attestation verify \\"$FILE\\" --repo orion-belt-dev/orion-belt\\n```\\n\\nAnd an image:\\n\\n```bash\\ngh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt-server:1.5.0 \\\\\\n  --repo orion-belt-dev/orion-belt\\n```\\n\\nThe images also carry BuildKit provenance and an SBOM. The full walkthrough,\\nincluding GPG, is in [Verify a release](/docs/quickstart/verify-release).\\n\\nIf you install from [pkgs.orion-belt.dev](https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev), your\\npackage manager already checks the GPG signature on every package.\\n\\n## Why only from 1.5.0\\n\\nProvenance has to be produced at build time. We could generate statements for\\n1.4.0 and earlier now, but they would describe builds that never happened that\\nway. Older releases keep their GPG signatures and nothing more.\\n\\n## The rest of the work\\n\\nProvenance was the visible part of a wider pass over how the project is built\\nand maintained:\\n\\n- Every GitHub Action is pinned to a commit SHA (except the SLSA generator,\\n  which has to be referenced by its release tag for `slsa-verifier` to trust\\n  it). Container base images in the lab are pinned by digest, and workflow\\n  tokens get write access only in the jobs that need it.\\n- `master` needs two approving reviews, a fresh approval after the last push,\\n  and nine passing CI checks, including a gate that fails on any known\\n  vulnerability in a dependency.\\n- Go fuzz targets cover scope parsing, SCP file names, shell word splitting\\n  and password hash verification. The last one found a panic on malformed\\n  stored hashes, fixed in 1.5.0.\\n- A [security policy](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/blob/master/SECURITY.md)\\n  with private reporting through GitHub advisories or\\n  maintainers@orion-belt.dev.\\n\\nThe project\'s\\n[OpenSSF Scorecard](https://scorecard.dev/viewer/?uri=github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)\\nwent from 7.1 to 9.4, and it holds the\\n[OpenSSF Best Practices](https://www.bestpractices.dev/projects/16726) passing\\nbadge. Scores aren\'t security, but they are a public checklist anyone can\\nre-run against us.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Orion Belt v1.5](/blog/orion-belt-v1-5)\\n- [Linux packages and labs](/blog/linux-packages-and-labs)"},{"id":"orion-belt-sdks","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-sdks","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-18-orion-belt-sdks.md","title":"Automating Orion Belt from Python and TypeScript","description":"Orion Belt 1.5.0 ships Python and TypeScript SDKs that cover every API endpoint, with no runtime dependencies. How they stay complete, how to log in with an SSH key, and a JIT access script to start from.","date":"2026-11-18T11:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":2.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-sdks","title":"Automating Orion Belt from Python and TypeScript","description":"Orion Belt 1.5.0 ships Python and TypeScript SDKs that cover every API endpoint, with no runtime dependencies. How they stay complete, how to log in with an SSH key, and a JIT access script to start from.","date":"2026-11-18T11:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","ops"],"keywords":["PAM API","Python PAM SDK","TypeScript PAM SDK","JIT access automation","SSH access API","Orion Belt SDK"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to check that an Orion Belt binary is the one we built","permalink":"/blog/verifiable-releases"},"nextItem":{"title":"Secure RDP access without exposing port 3389 \u2014 VPN, RD Gateway and reverse tunnels compared","permalink":"/blog/secure-rdp-without-exposing-3389"}},"content":"Most of what people build around a PAM gateway is glue: a chat bot that\\nfiles access requests, a nightly job that reviews standing grants, a deploy\\npipeline that asks for an hour on production. With **1.5.0**, that glue can\\nbe written against a typed client in Python or TypeScript instead of\\nhand-rolled HTTP calls.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n```sh\\npip install orion-belt-sdk        # Python 3.11+\\nnpm install @orion-belt/sdk       # Node 20+, Deno, Bun, browsers\\n```\\n\\n## Complete by construction\\n\\nAn SDK that covers \\"the main endpoints\\" quietly falls behind. These two cover\\nevery `/api/v1` route, and they stay that way because the build says so.\\n\\nThe server\'s routes are generated from the Go router into\\n[`sdk/routes.json`](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/blob/master/sdk/routes.json).\\nA server test fails if the router and the file disagree, and each SDK has a\\ncontract test that fails if a route has no method. Adding an endpoint without\\nadding it to both clients turns CI red.\\n\\nBoth clients follow the Go SDK\'s method names (`list_machines` in Python,\\n`listMachines` in TypeScript), so code ports between the three with little\\nthought.\\n\\n## No dependencies\\n\\nThe Python client uses only the standard library. The TypeScript client uses\\nthe platform `fetch`. Neither pulls anything into your dependency tree, which\\nmatters for a tool that holds admin credentials to your access gateway.\\n\\n## Log in with an SSH key\\n\\nScripts don\'t need a stored password. Prove you hold a key registered to the\\nuser, and the client keeps the API key it gets back:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization\\nfrom orion_belt_sdk import OrionBeltClient\\n\\nkey = serialization.load_ssh_private_key(open(\\"id_ed25519\\", \\"rb\\").read(), password=None)\\n\\n\\nclass Signer:\\n    public_key = key.public_key().public_bytes(\\n        serialization.Encoding.OpenSSH, serialization.PublicFormat.OpenSSH\\n    ).decode()\\n\\n    def sign(self, data: bytes) -> tuple[str, bytes]:\\n        return \\"ssh-ed25519\\", key.sign(data)\\n\\n\\nclient = OrionBeltClient(\\"https://orion.example.com\\")\\nclient.login_with_ssh_key(\\"deploy-bot\\", Signer())\\n```\\n\\n`cryptography` is only needed for this example; the SDK itself doesn\'t\\nrequire it. Password and TOTP login, session tokens and JWTs work too. See\\n[SDKs](/docs/api/sdks#authentication).\\n\\n## Example: ask for an hour on a machine\\n\\nA deploy job that requests just-in-time access, then waits for a human to\\napprove it:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { OrionBeltClient } from \'@orion-belt/sdk\';\\n\\nconst client = new OrionBeltClient({\\n  baseUrl: \'https://orion.example.com\',\\n  apiKey: process.env.ORION_API_KEY,\\n});\\n\\nconst machine = await client.getMachineByName(\'web-01\');\\n\\nconst request = await client.createAccessRequest({\\n  machine_id: machine.id,\\n  reason: `deploy ${process.env.GIT_SHA}`,\\n  duration: 3600,\\n  remote_users: [\'deploy\'],\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(`Access request ${request.id} is waiting for approval`);\\n```\\n\\nThe approver sees the request, its reason and its\\n[risk signals](/docs/guides/risk-and-anomalies) in the console or in chat,\\nand the access expires on its own an hour later.\\n\\n## License\\n\\nThe SDKs are AGPL v3.0, like the rest of Orion Belt. If you bundle one into\\nan application you distribute or serve to others, check that your license is\\ncompatible. [More on the license change](/blog/orion-belt-agpl).\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [SDK reference](/docs/api/sdks)\\n- [JIT access approvals](/blog/jit-access-approvals)\\n- [Orion Belt v1.5](/blog/orion-belt-v1-5)"},{"id":"secure-rdp-without-exposing-3389","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/secure-rdp-without-exposing-3389","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-11-secure-rdp-without-exposing-3389.md","title":"Secure RDP access without exposing port 3389 \u2014 VPN, RD Gateway and reverse tunnels compared","description":"Exposing RDP on port 3389 to the internet invites brute force and exploits. Compare the alternatives \u2014 VPN, Microsoft RD Gateway, SSH tunnels and an outbound reverse tunnel \u2014 and see how Orion Belt 1.4 brokers RDP and VNC with approvals and audit, without inbound ports.","date":"2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"RDP and VNC","permalink":"/blog/tags/rdp","description":"Remote desktop access through the gateway"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"}],"readingTime":3.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"secure-rdp-without-exposing-3389","title":"Secure RDP access without exposing port 3389 \u2014 VPN, RD Gateway and reverse tunnels compared","description":"Exposing RDP on port 3389 to the internet invites brute force and exploits. Compare the alternatives \u2014 VPN, Microsoft RD Gateway, SSH tunnels and an outbound reverse tunnel \u2014 and see how Orion Belt 1.4 brokers RDP and VNC with approvals and audit, without inbound ports.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["rdp","security","pam"],"keywords":["secure RDP access","RDP without port 3389","RDP gateway alternative","RDP over SSH tunnel","VNC remote access security","just-in-time RDP access"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Automating Orion Belt from Python and TypeScript","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-sdks"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to give engineers temporary Kubernetes access with expiring RoleBindings","permalink":"/blog/temporary-kubernetes-access-rolebindings"}},"content":"**The safest way to offer RDP is to not listen for it on the internet at\\nall.** An RDP port open to the world gets scanned and brute-forced\\ncontinuously, and RDP has had remote code execution bugs severe enough to be\\nwormable (BlueKeep,\\n[CVE-2019-0708](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-0708)). This post\\ncompares the common ways to put something in front of RDP and VNC, and where\\nOrion Belt 1.4 fits.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The options\\n\\n| Approach | Inbound port on the target network | Per-machine access | Approval / time limit | Audit of who connected |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| RDP open to the internet | 3389 on every host | Windows login only | No | Windows event log |\\n| VPN, then RDP | VPN port | Network-wide | No | VPN logs + event log |\\n| Microsoft RD Gateway | 443 on the gateway | Resource policies | No | Gateway logs |\\n| SSH tunnel through a bastion | 22 on the bastion | What the bastion allows | No | Bastion logs |\\n| **Outbound reverse tunnel (Orion Belt)** | **None** | **Grant per machine** | **Yes** | **Gateway audit log** |\\n\\n### VPN\\n\\nPuts users on the network, then they RDP directly. Simple, but access is\\nnetwork-wide: anyone on the VPN can try RDP against anything they can route\\nto. Fine for small teams; weak least privilege.\\n\\n### RD Gateway\\n\\nMicrosoft\'s\\n[Remote Desktop Gateway](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/remote/remote-desktop-services/remote-desktop-gateway-role)\\nwraps RDP in HTTPS and applies connection and resource authorization\\npolicies. It\'s the standard choice in Windows-centric shops. It still needs\\nan inbound HTTPS endpoint, a Windows Server to run it, and has no\\nrequest-and-approve flow.\\n\\n### SSH tunnel through a bastion\\n\\n`ssh -L 13389:win-01:3389 bastion` works anywhere OpenSSH does. The bastion\\nneeds to reach the Windows host, and nothing records who opened which tunnel\\nbeyond sshd logs.\\n\\n### Outbound reverse tunnel\\n\\nAn agent on the target network dials **out** to a gateway. Users connect to\\nthe gateway, which checks their grant and relays the stream through the\\nagent\'s existing connection. The target network opens no inbound port at all.\\n\\n## How it works in Orion Belt 1.4\\n\\nRDP and VNC are access types of their own, `rdp` and `vnc`. A grant for one\\ncovers that protocol only, and like SSH access it can be requested just in\\ntime:\\n\\n```bash\\nosh rdp win-01 -r --reason \\"patch Tuesday\\" -d 1800   # request, wait for approval\\nosh rdp win-01                                       # prints e.g. 127.0.0.1:53211\\n```\\n\\nPoint mstsc, Remmina, FreeRDP or any VNC viewer at the printed address.\\nPlain OpenSSH works too:\\n\\n```bash\\nssh -N -L 13389:win-01:3389 alice@orion-gateway.example.com\\n```\\n\\n**The agent decides where traffic goes.** By default it relays RDP to\\n`127.0.0.1:3389` and VNC to `127.0.0.1:5900` on its own host. Its config can\\npoint a protocol at another address on its network, or turn it off:\\n\\n```yaml\\nagent:\\n  name: win-01\\n  tunnels:\\n    rdp: 10.0.4.20:3389   # a Windows desktop next to the agent\\n    vnc: \\"off\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe gateway only names the protocol, so it can never steer an agent at an\\naddress its config doesn\'t list.\\n\\n**Windows hosts today:** the agent doesn\'t build for Windows yet. Run it on a\\nLinux machine on the same network and point `tunnels.rdp` at the Windows\\nhost.\\n\\n## What gets audited\\n\\nEach connection writes `tunnel.open` and `tunnel.close` entries (protocol,\\nmachine, bytes each way, duration); refused attempts write `tunnel.denied`.\\nThey appear in the [compliance reports](/docs/guides/compliance-reports) as\\n`rdp_session` and `vnc_session` rows.\\n\\nRDP and VNC sessions are **not recorded** yet. The stream is encrypted end\\nto end between your client and the target; recording it would need the\\ngateway to terminate the protocol. Keep that in mind if screen recording is a\\nhard requirement.\\n\\n## Hardening RDP itself\\n\\nWhatever is in front, keep these on:\\n\\n- **Network Level Authentication (NLA)**, so authentication happens before a\\n  full session is set up.\\n- **Account lockout** for repeated failures.\\n- **Firewall rules** that accept 3389 only from the relay (here, the agent\'s\\n  host, or `127.0.0.1` when the agent runs on the target).\\n- **Patching** on the RDP stack.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Is it safe to expose RDP to the internet?**\\nNo. Put a gateway, VPN or tunnel in front and accept RDP only from it.\\n\\n**Can I use RDP through an SSH tunnel?**\\nYes: forward a local port to the host\'s 3389 through an SSH server that can\\nreach it. Orion Belt accepts exactly that with plain `ssh -L`, and checks the\\nuser\'s `rdp` grant first.\\n\\n**Does this work for VNC?**\\nYes, the same way, on port 5900 with the `vnc` access type.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Orion Belt v1.4](/blog/orion-belt-v1-4)\\n- [RDP and VNC guide](/docs/guides/rdp-vnc)\\n- [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)"},{"id":"temporary-kubernetes-access-rolebindings","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/temporary-kubernetes-access-rolebindings","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-04-temporary-kubernetes-access-rolebindings.md","title":"How to give engineers temporary Kubernetes access with expiring RoleBindings","description":"A step-by-step guide to just-in-time Kubernetes access \u2014 why standing kubeconfigs are a risk, how expiring RoleBindings and impersonation work, and how Orion Belt 1.4 issues, reconciles and removes them through an in-cluster agent with no exposed API server.","date":"2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/blog/tags/kubernetes","description":"Just-in-time Kubernetes access"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":4.03,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"temporary-kubernetes-access-rolebindings","title":"How to give engineers temporary Kubernetes access with expiring RoleBindings","description":"A step-by-step guide to just-in-time Kubernetes access \u2014 why standing kubeconfigs are a risk, how expiring RoleBindings and impersonation work, and how Orion Belt 1.4 issues, reconciles and removes them through an in-cluster agent with no exposed API server.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["kubernetes","pam","security"],"keywords":["temporary Kubernetes access","just-in-time Kubernetes access","Kubernetes RoleBinding expiry","kubectl access approval","Kubernetes PAM","Kubernetes impersonation","zero standing privilege Kubernetes"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Secure RDP access without exposing port 3389 \u2014 VPN, RD Gateway and reverse tunnels compared","permalink":"/blog/secure-rdp-without-exposing-3389"},"nextItem":{"title":"Orion Belt v1.4 \u2014 RDP, VNC and just-in-time Kubernetes access","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-4"}},"content":"**Temporary Kubernetes access means a user gets a RoleBinding for a specific\\nnamespace and role, for a fixed time, and the binding is deleted when that\\ntime is up.** Kubernetes RBAC has no expiry field, so something outside the\\ncluster has to create and remove those bindings reliably. This guide shows\\nthe pattern and how Orion Belt 1.4 does it.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Why standing kubeconfigs are a problem\\n\\nThe usual setup: every engineer has a kubeconfig with a long-lived\\ncredential bound to a broad role, often `cluster-admin` \\"temporarily\\".\\n\\n- **Kubeconfigs get copied**: into CI variables, laptops, chat messages.\\n- **Bindings outlive their reason.** A role granted for an incident stays.\\n- **Revoking is manual**, and depends on someone remembering.\\n- **The API server is reachable** from wherever those laptops are.\\n\\n[Kubernetes\' own RBAC good practices](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/rbac-good-practices/)\\nrecommend least privilege and minimal use of cluster-admin. Expiring access\\nis how you get there without slowing people down.\\n\\n## The pattern\\n\\n1. A user requests a role in a namespace (`payments/edit`) with a reason and\\n   a duration.\\n2. Someone approves.\\n3. A controller creates a RoleBinding for that user, labelled with its\\n   expiry.\\n4. kubectl traffic reaches the API server as that user, so RBAC enforces\\n   exactly the binding.\\n5. When the time is up, the binding is deleted, even if the system that\\n   granted it is down.\\n\\nSteps 4 and 5 are where home-grown versions usually fall short.\\n\\n## How Orion Belt does it\\n\\n**Grants** have access type `k8s` and scopes of the form `namespace/role`.\\n`payments/edit` binds the `edit` ClusterRole in `payments` as a RoleBinding;\\n`*/view` binds `view` cluster-wide as a ClusterRoleBinding.\\n\\n**Subjects** are Kubernetes users named `orion:<username>`, so a binding\\nissued by Orion Belt can never match an identity from another authenticator.\\n\\n**An agent runs inside the cluster** and dials out to the gateway, like the\\nagents on hosts. The API server is never exposed.\\n\\n**kubectl goes through `osh kube`.** The agent forwards requests to the API\\nserver with its service account and `Impersonate-User: orion:<user>`,\\nafter stripping any credentials or impersonation headers the client sent.\\nRBAC then allows exactly what the issued bindings allow\\n([user impersonation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/authentication/#user-impersonation)).\\n\\n**Reconciling** keeps the cluster matched to the grants. The gateway\\ncompares the bindings it manages (named `orion-*`, labelled\\n`app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=orion-belt`) with active grants and creates,\\nupdates or deletes to match: when a grant changes, when the agent connects,\\nand every 30 seconds.\\n\\n**The agent enforces expiry on its own.** It deletes expired bindings every\\nminute, even while the gateway is unreachable.\\n\\n## Step by step\\n\\n**1. Install the in-cluster agent.** Register a machine for the cluster in\\nthe console (its name is the cluster\'s name in grants), then:\\n\\n```bash\\nkubectl create namespace orion-belt\\nkubectl -n orion-belt create secret generic orion-belt-agent-key \\\\\\n  --from-file=agent_key=./agent-key\\nkubectl apply -f orion-belt-agent.yaml   # from deploy/kubernetes/, with server and agent name set\\n```\\n\\nThe manifest\'s ClusterRole lets the agent manage bindings, `bind`\\nClusterRoles and `impersonate` users. Narrow `bind` with `resourceNames` to\\nthe roles you allow (for example `view`, `edit`).\\n\\n**2. Request access.**\\n\\n```bash\\nosh kube prod-eu -r --scope payments/edit --reason \\"INC-42\\" -d 1800\\n# Kubernetes API of prod-eu relayed on 127.0.0.1:53817\\n# export KUBECONFIG=/home/alice/.orion-belt/kube/prod-eu.yaml\\n```\\n\\n**3. Use kubectl normally.**\\n\\n```bash\\nkubectl get pods             # namespace defaults to the first scope\'s\\nkubectl -n default get pods  # Forbidden: outside the grant\\n```\\n\\nThe generated kubeconfig holds no credentials. It points at a loopback relay,\\nand the gateway authenticates the SSH connection behind it.\\n\\n**4. Check for drift.**\\n\\n```bash\\noadmin machines kube-reconcile prod-eu          # report only\\noadmin machines kube-reconcile prod-eu --apply  # fix now\\n```\\n\\n## Audit trail\\n\\nEvery step is logged: the request and approval (`access_type: k8s` with its\\nscopes), each binding created, updated or deleted with the reason\\n(`granted`, `expired`, `revoked`, `orphaned`), and each kubectl relay\\nconnection. The [compliance reports](/docs/guides/compliance-reports) list\\nthem as `k8s_session` rows.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Can a Kubernetes RoleBinding expire on its own?**\\nNo. RBAC objects have no expiry. A controller has to delete them; Orion Belt\\ndoes it from both the gateway and the in-cluster agent.\\n\\n**Does this work with managed clusters (EKS, GKE, AKS)?**\\nThe agent only uses standard Kubernetes RBAC and impersonation through its\\nown service account, nothing provider-specific, and needs outbound access to\\nthe gateway. Our end-to-end tests run on kind; tell us how it goes on a\\nmanaged cluster.\\n\\n**Does the user need a kubeconfig from the cloud provider?**\\nNo. `osh kube` writes one that points at the local relay.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Orion Belt v1.4](/blog/orion-belt-v1-4)\\n- [Kubernetes guide](/docs/guides/kubernetes)\\n- [What is privileged access management?](/blog/what-is-privileged-access-management)"},{"id":"orion-belt-v1-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-24-orion-belt-v1-4.md","title":"Orion Belt v1.4 \u2014 RDP, VNC and just-in-time Kubernetes access","description":"Orion Belt 1.4.0 adds RDP and VNC through the reverse agent tunnel, just-in-time Kubernetes RoleBindings through an in-cluster agent, compliance reports, and risk signals with access explain and anomaly flags.","date":"2026-10-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":1.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-v1-4","title":"Orion Belt v1.4 \u2014 RDP, VNC and just-in-time Kubernetes access","description":"Orion Belt 1.4.0 adds RDP and VNC through the reverse agent tunnel, just-in-time Kubernetes RoleBindings through an in-cluster agent, compliance reports, and risk signals with access explain and anomaly flags.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","self-hosted","security"],"keywords":["Orion Belt 1.4","RDP gateway","VNC gateway","Kubernetes JIT access","RoleBinding","PAM compliance reports"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to give engineers temporary Kubernetes access with expiring RoleBindings","permalink":"/blog/temporary-kubernetes-access-rolebindings"},"nextItem":{"title":"Orion Belt 1.3 \u2014 signed packages for every Linux distribution, clients for every OS","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-3-packages-everywhere"}},"content":"**Orion Belt 1.4.0** takes the outbound-agent model beyond SSH. RDP, VNC and\\n`kubectl` now go through the same agents that dial out to the gateway, with\\nthe same request, approve and expire flow and the same audit trail.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## RDP and VNC\\n\\n`osh rdp win-01` and `osh vnc lab-02` open a local port relayed to the\\nmachine through its agent; point any RDP or VNC client at it. Plain\\n`ssh -N -L 13389:win-01:3389 alice@gateway` works too. Grants use the new\\n`rdp` and `vnc` access types, and the agent decides where each protocol goes,\\nso the gateway can never steer it at an arbitrary host. See\\n[RDP and VNC](/docs/guides/rdp-vnc).\\n\\n## Just-in-time Kubernetes access\\n\\nAn approved `k8s` grant, scoped as `payments/edit` or `*/view`, becomes a\\nreal RoleBinding or ClusterRoleBinding for `orion:<user>`, issued by an agent\\nrunning inside the cluster. `osh kube prod-eu` writes a credential-free\\nkubeconfig and relays kubectl as that user, so Kubernetes RBAC enforces\\nexactly the grant. Bindings are reconciled against grants continuously,\\nsurvive gateway restarts, and are deleted by the agent itself when they\\nexpire, even if the gateway is unreachable. See\\n[Kubernetes](/docs/guides/kubernetes).\\n\\n## Compliance and access intelligence\\n\\n- [Compliance reports](/docs/guides/compliance-reports): SOC 2 access,\\n  ISO 27001 access review and PCI admin activity, as CSV, PDF or JSON. They\\n  include RDP, VNC and kubectl connections.\\n- [Risk signals, access explain and anomaly flags](/docs/guides/risk-and-anomalies):\\n  every request records who, from where and how; a risk policy can escalate\\n  or auto-approve; `osh explain` says why access is allowed or denied; and\\n  unusual sessions are flagged for review.\\n\\n## Upgrade notes\\n\\n- `both` grants now cover `ssh` and `scp` only. Before 1.4.0 the permission\\n  check matched `both` for any access type; with tunnel and Kubernetes types\\n  added, that would have widened every existing SSH grant.\\n- Agents must be on 1.4.0 for RDP, VNC and Kubernetes. Older agents keep\\n  working for SSH and are told to upgrade if a tunnel is requested.\\n\\nFull notes are on the\\n[v1.4.0 release](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.4.0)."},{"id":"orion-belt-v1-3-packages-everywhere","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-3-packages-everywhere","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-23-orion-belt-v1-3-packages-everywhere.md","title":"Orion Belt 1.3 \u2014 signed packages for every Linux distribution, clients for every OS","description":"Orion Belt 1.3 publishes signed apt, dnf, zypper, apk and pacman repositories at pkgs.orion-belt.dev across nine CPU architectures, clients for macOS, Windows and the BSDs, a one-line installer, an access capability catalog in the console, and gateway hardening.","date":"2026-10-23T12:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux","description":"Linux packages, distributions and host tooling"}],"readingTime":3.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-v1-3-packages-everywhere","title":"Orion Belt 1.3 \u2014 signed packages for every Linux distribution, clients for every OS","description":"Orion Belt 1.3 publishes signed apt, dnf, zypper, apk and pacman repositories at pkgs.orion-belt.dev across nine CPU architectures, clients for macOS, Windows and the BSDs, a one-line installer, an access capability catalog in the console, and gateway hardening.","date":"2026-10-23T12:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ops","self-hosted","linux"],"keywords":["Orion Belt 1.3","SSH gateway apt repository","PAM rpm package","apk SSH bastion","pacman SSH gateway","pkgs.orion-belt.dev","install SSH bastion Linux"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt v1.4 \u2014 RDP, VNC and just-in-time Kubernetes access","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"SSH key management at scale \u2014 authorized_keys sprawl, certificates and gateways compared","permalink":"/blog/ssh-key-management-at-scale"}},"content":"**Orion Belt 1.3.0** (October 22, with 1.3.1 the next day) makes installing\\nthe gateway, the agent and the clients a package manager command on almost\\nany machine. Everything comes from signed repositories at\\n**[pkgs.orion-belt.dev](https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev)**, so upgrades arrive with\\nthe rest of your system updates.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Install in one line\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -fsSL https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev/install.sh | sh                # clients: osh, ocp, oadmin\\ncurl -fsSL https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev/install.sh | sh -s -- agent    # agent (Linux)\\ncurl -fsSL https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev/install.sh | sh -s -- server   # server (Linux)\\n```\\n\\n```powershell\\nirm https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev/install.ps1 | iex                     # clients on Windows\\n```\\n\\nOn Linux the script adds the repository for your package manager and\\ninstalls from it. Every download is checked against the release\'s\\n`checksums.txt`, and its GPG signature is checked when `gpg` is installed. If\\nyou\'d rather not pipe to a shell, add the repository by hand; the\\n[Get started](/docs/quickstart/get-started) guide has the commands per OS.\\n\\n## What\'s covered\\n\\n| Platform | Package format | Architectures |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Debian, Ubuntu, Raspberry Pi OS | `.deb` (apt) | amd64, arm64, armhf, armel, i386, ppc64el, s390x, riscv64, loong64 |\\n| Fedora, RHEL, Rocky, Alma, Amazon Linux | `.rpm` (dnf/yum) | x86_64, aarch64, armv7hl, armv6hl, i386, ppc64le, s390x, riscv64, loongarch64 |\\n| openSUSE, SLES | `.rpm` (zypper) | as above |\\n| Alpine | `.apk` | x86_64, aarch64, armv7, armhf, x86, ppc64le, s390x, riscv64, loongarch64 |\\n| Arch Linux, Arch Linux ARM, Arch Linux 32 | pacman | x86_64, aarch64, armv7h, i686 |\\n| macOS | archive | amd64, arm64 (clients) |\\n| Windows | zip, scoop | amd64, arm64, 386 (clients) |\\n| FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD, DragonFly, illumos, Solaris | archive | clients |\\n| Containers (GHCR) | image | amd64, arm64, arm/v7, arm/v6, 386, ppc64le, s390x, riscv64 |\\n\\nThree packages per Linux platform: `orion-belt` (gateway),\\n`orion-belt-agent` and `orion-belt-tools` (`osh`, `ocp`, `oadmin`). The\\nserver and agent packages work with systemd, OpenRC, runit and SysV init.\\n\\n## Why so many architectures\\n\\nAgents run where your machines are, and that\'s not only x86 servers:\\nRaspberry Pis in a lab, ARM boards at the edge, s390x and ppc64le in older\\ndata centers. The agent is a single Go binary with no runtime dependencies,\\nso building it for nine CPUs costs CI minutes, not maintenance.\\n\\n## Signed end to end\\n\\n- The apt, dnf, zypper and pacman repositories are signed with the release\\n  GPG key.\\n- `.deb` files carry an embedded signature and `.rpm` files a native one.\\n- Every release file has a detached `.sig`, and `checksums.txt` is signed.\\n\\nThe key fingerprint is on the front page of\\n[pkgs.orion-belt.dev](https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev); compare it before you trust\\nthe repository.\\n\\n## Also in 1.3\\n\\n- **Capability catalog in the console** (#57), building on the access\\n  catalog from 1.2.1: users see what they can request.\\n- **CLI coverage** for the endpoints added since 1.0 (#64).\\n- **Gateway hardening**: plugin settings validate admin-set URLs, paths and\\n  mail headers; Slack `response_url` posts are restricted; users can only\\n  delete their own SSH keys.\\n- **Exec fixes**: one-shot commands (`ssh user+web-01@gateway uptime`) run on\\n  the machine named in the SSH username, no longer lose output, and no longer\\n  hang when the command fails. A shell\'s last output arrives before the\\n  session ends.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Is there an apt repository for Orion Belt?**\\nYes: `deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/orion-belt.gpg] https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev stable main`,\\nafter downloading the key from `https://pkgs.orion-belt.dev/orion-belt.gpg`.\\n\\n**Does the agent run on a Raspberry Pi?**\\nYes. The `armhf` build runs on Raspberry Pi OS, and `arm64` on 64-bit Pi OS.\\n\\n**Can I run the gateway in Docker instead?**\\nYes. Server and agent images are on GHCR for eight architectures.\\n\\nRelease notes:\\n[v1.3.0](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.3.0) \xb7\\n[v1.3.1](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.3.1)"},{"id":"ssh-key-management-at-scale","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/ssh-key-management-at-scale","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-14-ssh-key-management-at-scale.md","title":"SSH key management at scale \u2014 authorized_keys sprawl, certificates and gateways compared","description":"Three ways to manage SSH access across a fleet \u2014 distributing authorized_keys, SSH certificates from a CA, and an access gateway \u2014 with how each handles onboarding, offboarding, rotation and audit, and when to combine them.","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":3.83,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ssh-key-management-at-scale","title":"SSH key management at scale \u2014 authorized_keys sprawl, certificates and gateways compared","description":"Three ways to manage SSH access across a fleet \u2014 distributing authorized_keys, SSH certificates from a CA, and an access gateway \u2014 with how each handles onboarding, offboarding, rotation and audit, and when to combine them.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","security","ops"],"keywords":["SSH key management","authorized_keys management","SSH certificates","SSH CA","SSH key rotation","offboarding SSH keys","SSH key sprawl"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt 1.3 \u2014 signed packages for every Linux distribution, clients for every OS","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-3-packages-everywhere"},"nextItem":{"title":"Self-service SSH access requests \u2014 let engineers see what they can ask for","permalink":"/blog/self-service-ssh-access-requests"}},"content":"**SSH key sprawl is what happens when every person\'s public key is copied\\ninto `authorized_keys` on every server they ever needed.** Nobody knows\\nwhich keys are still in use, offboarding means editing hundreds of files,\\nand a key from a laptop retired three years ago still opens production.\\nThere are three ways out. This post compares them.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The three models\\n\\n| | `authorized_keys` distribution | SSH certificates (CA) | Access gateway |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Where trust lives | Each host\'s key files | The CA public key on each host | The gateway |\\n| Onboarding | Push key to N hosts | Issue a certificate | Create user + grant |\\n| Offboarding | Remove key from N hosts | Stop issuing; old certs expire | Disable user; access ends now |\\n| Credential lifetime | Forever | Hours, by design | Session; grants can expire |\\n| Per-machine access | Which hosts got the key | Principals in the cert | Grants per machine |\\n| Approval workflow | None | None built in | Built in |\\n| Session recording | No | No | Yes |\\n| Host changes needed | Every change | Once (`TrustedUserCAKeys`) | Agent install, once |\\n\\n## Model 1: distributing authorized_keys\\n\\nConfig management (Ansible, Puppet, Chef) pushes keys to hosts. It works,\\nand most teams start here.\\n\\nIt breaks down on three things:\\n\\n- **Offboarding is a deploy.** Until the run reaches every host, the key\\n  still works. Hosts that miss the run keep it.\\n- **Keys never expire.** OpenSSH supports an `expiry-time` option in\\n  `authorized_keys`, but almost nobody uses it.\\n- **No record of use.** You know who *could* log in, not who did.\\n\\nIf you stay here, at least set `LogLevel VERBOSE` in sshd so logins record\\nthe key fingerprint, and audit keys against your directory regularly.\\n\\n## Model 2: SSH certificates\\n\\nOpenSSH has supported certificates since 5.4. A CA signs a user\'s public key\\nwith a validity window and a list of principals (the accounts it may log in\\nas). Hosts trust the CA, not individual keys\\n([ssh-keygen(1) CERTIFICATES](https://man.openbsd.org/ssh-keygen#CERTIFICATES)).\\n\\n```text\\n# On each host, once:\\nTrustedUserCAKeys /etc/ssh/user_ca.pub\\n\\n# Issuing a cert valid for 8 hours, as user \\"deploy\\":\\nssh-keygen -s user_ca -I alice@example.com -n deploy -V +8h id_ed25519.pub\\n```\\n\\nCertificates fix expiry and offboarding: stop issuing and access ends when\\nthe last certificate runs out. Host certificates fix the other direction,\\nreplacing \\"trust on first use\\" with a CA-signed host key.\\n\\nWhat they don\'t give you: an approval step, a record of the session, or\\nper-request scoping, unless you build the issuing service yourself. The CA\\nkey also becomes the most sensitive secret you own.\\n\\n## Model 3: an access gateway\\n\\nUsers authenticate to a gateway; the gateway decides per request whether they\\nmay reach a machine as a given account, then brokers the session. Users\'\\nkeys never need to be on the targets.\\n\\nWith Orion Belt:\\n\\n- Users register their own keys with the gateway (`osh keys add`), and\\n  remove them the same way.\\n- Grants are per user, machine and remote user. Privileged ones are\\n  requested [just in time](/blog/jit-access-approvals) and expire.\\n- Every session is recorded at the gateway.\\n- Targets run an agent that [dials out](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents); you\\n  don\'t manage keys on them at all.\\n\\n## Combining them\\n\\nThe models stack well. Orion Belt includes an\\n[optional SSH CA](/blog/optional-ssh-ca): it issues short-lived user\\ncertificates for operators and host certificates for the gateway and agents.\\nThat gives you short-lived credentials (model 2) behind a gateway with grants,\\napprovals and recording (model 3). Static `authorized_keys` remain only for\\nbreak-glass.\\n\\n## A migration path\\n\\n1. Turn on `LogLevel VERBOSE` and find out which keys are actually used.\\n2. Put a gateway in front of production and move people to it.\\n3. Turn on short-lived certificates.\\n4. Remove personal keys from `authorized_keys` on targets, host group by host\\n   group, keeping one documented break-glass key.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**How do I revoke an SSH key on all servers at once?**\\nWith `authorized_keys`, you can\'t, only push a change and wait. With\\ncertificates, use a revocation list (`RevokedKeys`) or wait for expiry. With a\\ngateway, disable the user or remove the key at the gateway, and it takes\\neffect on the next connection.\\n\\n**How often should SSH keys be rotated?**\\nRotation matters less when credentials are short-lived. With certificates\\nvalid for hours, the user key itself can stay put; protect it with a\\nhardware token instead.\\n\\n**Are SSH certificates better than keys?**\\nCertificates are keys plus a signature with an expiry and principals. They\\nfix expiry and offboarding; they don\'t add approvals or recording.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [SSH bastion host best practices](/blog/ssh-bastion-host-best-practices)\\n- [MFA and WebAuthn for SSH](/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh)"},{"id":"self-service-ssh-access-requests","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/self-service-ssh-access-requests","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-30-self-service-ssh-access-requests.md","title":"Self-service SSH access requests \u2014 let engineers see what they can ask for","description":"Just-in-time access only works if people know what to request. Orion Belt 1.2.1 adds an access catalog that shows each user what they can request, plus authentication and proxy hardening and a terminal resize fix.","date":"2026-09-30T12:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":2.59,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"self-service-ssh-access-requests","title":"Self-service SSH access requests \u2014 let engineers see what they can ask for","description":"Just-in-time access only works if people know what to request. Orion Belt 1.2.1 adds an access catalog that shows each user what they can request, plus authentication and proxy hardening and a terminal resize fix.","date":"2026-09-30T12:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","ssh","security"],"keywords":["self-service access request","SSH access request workflow","just-in-time SSH access","access catalog","Orion Belt 1.2.1"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SSH key management at scale \u2014 authorized_keys sprawl, certificates and gateways compared","permalink":"/blog/ssh-key-management-at-scale"},"nextItem":{"title":"Tracing SSH sessions with OpenTelemetry \u2014 finding the slow hop","permalink":"/blog/opentelemetry-ssh-tracing"}},"content":"**Just-in-time access fails quietly when engineers don\'t know what to ask\\nfor.** They guess machine names, request too broadly \\"to be safe\\", or ping an\\nadmin on chat, which is the workflow JIT was supposed to replace.\\n**Orion Belt 1.2.1**, released September 29, adds an access catalog to fix\\nthat.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The access catalog\\n\\nThe console now shows each user the capabilities they could request, based\\non their current relationships: which machines, as which remote users, with\\nwhich access types. Instead of typing `web-03` and hoping, they pick from a\\nlist that only contains things an approver could actually grant them.\\n\\nThat has a few effects:\\n\\n- **Fewer rejected requests.** Requests for machines a user has no path to\\n  don\'t get filed.\\n- **Narrower requests.** When the exact option is visible, people stop asking\\n  for more than they need.\\n- **No information leak.** The catalog is computed per user, from the same\\n  relationships the authorization check uses\\n  ([ReBAC](/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines)), so it doesn\'t reveal machines a\\n  user has no business knowing about.\\n\\n## How a request flows\\n\\n1. The engineer picks a target from the catalog, adds a reason and a\\n   duration, and submits. Or from the CLI:\\n   `osh --request-access deploy@web-03 --reason \\"INC-1234\\" --duration 1800`,\\n   then `osh requests list` to follow it.\\n2. Approvers are notified in the console bell and, with the chat plugins, in\\n   Slack, Discord, Microsoft Teams or Rocket.Chat, where they can approve in\\n   place.\\n3. On approval the grant is active for the requested duration, then expires\\n   without anyone revoking it.\\n4. The session is recorded and tied to the request in the audit log.\\n\\nDetail on the approval side: [JIT access approvals](/blog/jit-access-approvals).\\n\\n## Also in 1.2.1\\n\\n- **Authentication, authorization and proxy hardening** across the gateway\\n  (PR #99).\\n- **Terminal resize fix**: window size changes now reach the target\'s PTY, so\\n  full-screen programs (`htop`, `vim`, `less`) redraw correctly when you\\n  resize your terminal (#103).\\n- **Tracing fix**: the OTLP/HTTP traces path is set explicitly, so HTTP\\n  collectors receive spans (PR #101).\\n- Dependency updates, including gRPC and the OpenTelemetry SDK.\\n\\n## Designing a request policy that people follow\\n\\nA catalog helps; the policy around it matters more.\\n\\n| Decision | Suggested default |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| What needs a request | Production and anything holding customer data |\\n| What stays standing | Dev and staging, for the team that owns them |\\n| Default duration | 1 hour; 8 hours maximum without a second approver |\\n| Reason field | Required; link a ticket or incident |\\n| Approvers | Not the requester; two people for the most sensitive systems |\\n\\nKeep the request faster than the workaround. If requesting access takes\\nlonger than finding a shared key in someone\'s dotfiles, people will find the\\nkey.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**What is a self-service access request?**\\nA request a user files themselves for specific, time-limited access, which an\\napprover accepts or rejects, instead of asking an admin to change permissions\\nby hand.\\n\\n**Can approvals happen in Slack?**\\nYes. The chat plugins post requests to Slack, Discord, Teams or Rocket.Chat\\nwith approve and reject actions.\\n\\nRelease notes:\\n[v1.2.1](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.2.1)"},{"id":"opentelemetry-ssh-tracing","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/opentelemetry-ssh-tracing","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-16-opentelemetry-ssh-tracing.md","title":"Tracing SSH sessions with OpenTelemetry \u2014 finding the slow hop","description":"How Orion Belt traces an SSH session across the gateway, the agent and the target with OpenTelemetry \u2014 the span tree, how W3C trace context crosses an SSH channel, what is kept out of spans, and how to turn it on.","date":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Observability","permalink":"/blog/tags/observability","description":"Metrics, logs and tracing"},{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":3.08,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"opentelemetry-ssh-tracing","title":"Tracing SSH sessions with OpenTelemetry \u2014 finding the slow hop","description":"How Orion Belt traces an SSH session across the gateway, the agent and the target with OpenTelemetry \u2014 the span tree, how W3C trace context crosses an SSH channel, what is kept out of spans, and how to turn it on.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["observability","ssh","ops"],"keywords":["OpenTelemetry SSH","trace SSH sessions","OTLP SSH gateway","SSH latency","distributed tracing bastion","traceparent SSH"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Self-service SSH access requests \u2014 let engineers see what they can ask for","permalink":"/blog/self-service-ssh-access-requests"},"nextItem":{"title":"What is privileged access management (PAM)? A plain-English guide for infrastructure teams","permalink":"/blog/what-is-privileged-access-management"}},"content":"**When an SSH login through a gateway takes eight seconds, the question is\\nwhich hop is slow: authorization, the gateway \u2192 agent tunnel, or the agent \u2192\\ntarget connection.** Logs and metrics don\'t answer that well. Distributed\\ntracing does. Since 1.1.0, Orion Belt emits OpenTelemetry traces for SSH\\nsessions across both processes.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Metrics, logs or traces?\\n\\n| Question | Use |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Is it up? How many sessions? Alert me | Metrics (`/metrics`, Prometheus) |\\n| What exactly happened in this session? | Logs and the session recording |\\n| **Which hop is slow or failing?** | **Traces** |\\n\\n## The span tree\\n\\nOne trace per SSH session, spanning the gateway and the agent:\\n\\n```text\\ngateway.ssh.session                (gateway, SERVER)\\n\u251C\u2500\u2500 gateway.authorize              machine lookup + permission check\\n\u251C\u2500\u2500 gateway.agent.open_channel     the gateway \u2192 agent hop (CLIENT)\\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 agent.session              (agent, SERVER, same trace)\\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 agent.target.shell     the agent \u2192 target hop\\n\u2502           or agent.target.exec   one-shot command / scp\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 gateway.proxy.shell            session body\\n    or gateway.proxy.exec\\n```\\n\\nConnect latency shows up in `gateway.authorize` and\\n`gateway.agent.open_channel`. The `proxy.*` and `target.shell` spans last as\\nlong as the user stays connected: read them as session duration, not\\nlatency.\\n\\nAttributes to filter on: `orion.user`, `orion.target.machine`,\\n`orion.target.remote_user`, and `orion.session.id`, which joins a trace to\\nthe session recording and the audit log.\\n\\n## Carrying trace context through SSH\\n\\nHTTP has headers for [W3C trace context](https://www.w3.org/TR/trace-context/).\\nSSH doesn\'t. The gateway puts the `traceparent` into the extra data of the\\nSSH channel-open request it sends to the agent, and the agent parents its\\nspans on it.\\n\\nThis is tolerant in both directions. An agent that predates tracing ignores\\nthe extra data. A new agent that receives none starts its own trace and marks\\nit with `orion.trace.linked_to_gateway=false`. A trace-context problem never\\nfails a session, so you can upgrade gateway and agents separately. That\\nattribute is also how you spot a half-upgraded fleet.\\n\\n## What stays out of spans\\n\\nSpans go to a collector, which usually has weaker access controls than\\nsession recordings. So commands are recorded as the program name only\\n(`orion.command`), never with arguments or `NAME=value` environment\\nprefixes. A `mysql -p...` typed by an admin doesn\'t end up in your tracing\\nbackend.\\n\\n## Turning it on\\n\\nBoth sides read the same block. You need both for a full trace.\\n\\n```yaml\\n# server.yaml and agent.yaml\\ntracing:\\n  enabled: true\\n  endpoint: \\"otel-collector:4317\\"   # gRPC wants host:port, not a URL\\n  protocol: grpc                    # or http\\n  insecure: false                   # true only for a trusted plaintext link\\n  sample_ratio: 1.0\\n```\\n\\nLeave `endpoint` empty to use the standard `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_*`\\nenvironment variables instead. Any OTLP backend works: the OpenTelemetry\\nCollector, Jaeger, Grafana Tempo, Honeycomb.\\n\\nService names default to `orion-belt-gateway` and\\n`orion-belt-agent-<agent name>`. The agent name is included on purpose: a\\nfleet that all reports as one service makes the agent hop useless to look at.\\n\\n## What it costs\\n\\nDisabled means free: no exporter, no tracer provider, one atomic load per\\nentry point. Measured with the project\'s benchmarks on an M1:\\n\\n| Path | Disabled | Enabled |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Start + End a span | 2.3 ns, 0 allocs | 644 ns, 3 allocs |\\n\\nA test in CI asserts the disabled path doesn\'t allocate.\\n\\n## Not traced (yet)\\n\\nThe HTTP API isn\'t instrumented; API requests carry a `request_id` for log\\ncorrelation instead. Web terminal sessions get the agent hop but no HTTP\\nparent span.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Can OpenTelemetry trace an SSH connection?**\\nNot out of the box: SSH has no header for trace context. A gateway that\\ncontrols both ends can carry it, as Orion Belt does in the channel-open data.\\n\\n**Does tracing record what users type?**\\nNo. Spans hold timing, identities and the program name. Keystrokes and output\\nare in the session recording, which has its own access controls.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Orion Belt 1.1 and 1.2](/blog/orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2)\\n- [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)"},{"id":"what-is-privileged-access-management","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/what-is-privileged-access-management","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-02-what-is-privileged-access-management.md","title":"What is privileged access management (PAM)? A plain-English guide for infrastructure teams","description":"Privileged access management (PAM) controls, approves, records and expires access to the accounts and systems that can do the most damage. What PAM covers, the terms (PASM, PEDM, JIT, standing privilege), and how it applies to SSH.","date":"2026-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"}],"readingTime":3.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"what-is-privileged-access-management","title":"What is privileged access management (PAM)? A plain-English guide for infrastructure teams","description":"Privileged access management (PAM) controls, approves, records and expires access to the accounts and systems that can do the most damage. What PAM covers, the terms (PASM, PEDM, JIT, standing privilege), and how it applies to SSH.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","security","ssh"],"keywords":["what is PAM","privileged access management","PAM meaning","PASM","PEDM","just-in-time access","standing privilege","SSH PAM"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Tracing SSH sessions with OpenTelemetry \u2014 finding the slow hop","permalink":"/blog/opentelemetry-ssh-tracing"},"nextItem":{"title":"SSH bastion host best practices \u2014 a hardening checklist","permalink":"/blog/ssh-bastion-host-best-practices"}},"content":"**Privileged access management (PAM) is the set of controls that decides\\nwho may use powerful accounts and systems, when, with whose approval, and\\nkeeps a record of what they did.** \\"Privileged\\" means access that can change\\nproduction, read sensitive data or turn off other controls: root on a server,\\na database admin login, a cloud admin role.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nPAM is not to be confused with Linux PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules),\\nthe library that handles logins on a Linux host. Same acronym, different\\nthing.\\n\\n## What PAM does\\n\\n| Capability | Question it answers |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| **Access control** | Who may reach which system, as which account? |\\n| **Approval** | Did someone else agree before access was granted? |\\n| **Time limits** | When does the access end? |\\n| **Credential protection** | Does the person ever see the password or key? |\\n| **Session recording** | What exactly happened during the session? |\\n| **Audit** | Can we prove all of the above to an auditor later? |\\n\\n## The vocabulary\\n\\n**Standing privilege**: access that is always on. A key in\\n`authorized_keys`, an admin group membership. It\'s the default almost\\neverywhere, and it\'s what attackers use after stealing a credential.\\n\\n**Just-in-time (JIT) access**: access granted for a task and removed\\nautomatically afterwards. The goal is zero standing privilege: nobody holds\\nproduction access until they need it.\\n[More on JIT approvals](/blog/jit-access-approvals).\\n\\n**PASM, privileged account and session management**: vaulting shared\\ncredentials, brokering sessions so the user never sees them, and recording\\nthose sessions.\\n\\n**PEDM, privilege elevation and delegation management**: controlling what a\\nuser may do once logged in, such as `sudo` policies and endpoint elevation.\\n\\n**Break-glass access**: an emergency path for when the normal one is down,\\nused rarely and audited heavily.\\n\\n**Least privilege**: the principle behind all of it. Each person gets the\\nminimum access needed, for the minimum time.\\n\\n## Why teams adopt PAM\\n\\n- **Stolen credentials are the common path in.** The\\n  [Verizon Data Breach Investigations Report](https://www.verizon.com/business/resources/reports/dbir/)\\n  has listed the use of stolen credentials among the top breach actions for\\n  years. Access that doesn\'t exist yet can\'t be stolen.\\n- **Auditors ask for it.** SOC 2 (CC6.1 to CC6.3), ISO 27001 (Annex A\\n  access control) and PCI DSS (requirements 7, 8 and 10) all ask who has\\n  access to what, how it was approved and how it is logged.\\n- **People leave.** Standing keys and group memberships survive offboarding\\n  far too often.\\n\\n## PAM for SSH specifically\\n\\nMost enterprise PAM suites started with Windows and vaulted passwords. For\\nLinux fleets, the privileged path is SSH, and it needs:\\n\\n1. **A single entry point** (a gateway or bastion) instead of direct SSH to\\n   each host.\\n2. **Per-machine grants** tied to a person, not a shared key.\\n3. **JIT requests** for production, with approval in the console or chat.\\n4. **Session recording** taken at the gateway, out of reach of root on the\\n   target.\\n5. **MFA** at the gateway.\\n6. **Short-lived credentials** (SSH certificates) instead of static keys.\\n\\nOrion Belt is a self-hosted SSH gateway built around those six. Targets run\\nan agent that [dials out](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents), so they need no\\ninbound SSH port.\\n\\n## PAM vs. related tools\\n\\n| Tool | Main job | Overlap with PAM |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Identity provider (IdP) | Who you are; SSO | Feeds PAM identities; doesn\'t grant server access |\\n| VPN | Network reachability | None at the account level |\\n| Bastion / jump host | One network entry point | The path, without approvals or recording ([comparison](/blog/bastion-vs-pam)) |\\n| Secrets manager | Storing credentials for apps | Overlaps with PASM vaulting |\\n| PAM | Who, when, approved by whom, recorded | \u2014 |\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Is PAM only for large enterprises?**\\nNo. The need starts when more than one person has production access. What\\nchanges with size is how much of it you automate.\\n\\n**What\'s the difference between PAM and IAM?**\\nIAM manages identities and their general access. PAM is the subset that\\ngoverns the highest-risk access more strictly: approval, time limits and\\nrecording.\\n\\n**Does PAM replace sudo policies?**\\nNo. PAM decides who gets onto the machine and as which account; sudo\\npolicies (PEDM) decide what that account may do there.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Open-source PAM and SSH bastion options compared](/blog/open-source-pam-landscape)\\n- [SSH bastion host best practices](/blog/ssh-bastion-host-best-practices)\\n- [ReBAC for SSH machines](/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines)"},{"id":"ssh-bastion-host-best-practices","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/ssh-bastion-host-best-practices","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-19-ssh-bastion-host-best-practices.md","title":"SSH bastion host best practices \u2014 a hardening checklist","description":"A checklist for running an SSH bastion or jump host safely in 2026 \u2014 identity, keys, MFA, network exposure, least privilege, session recording, logging and break-glass \u2014 with OpenSSH settings and where a PAM gateway replaces manual work.","date":"2026-08-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":4.14,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ssh-bastion-host-best-practices","title":"SSH bastion host best practices \u2014 a hardening checklist","description":"A checklist for running an SSH bastion or jump host safely in 2026 \u2014 identity, keys, MFA, network exposure, least privilege, session recording, logging and break-glass \u2014 with OpenSSH settings and where a PAM gateway replaces manual work.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","security","ops"],"keywords":["SSH bastion best practices","jump host hardening","bastion host security","SSH hardening checklist","secure SSH access","ProxyJump"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"What is privileged access management (PAM)? A plain-English guide for infrastructure teams","permalink":"/blog/what-is-privileged-access-management"},"nextItem":{"title":"Orion Belt 1.1 and 1.2 \u2014 tracing, report exports, usage analytics and a one-line installer","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2"}},"content":"**A bastion host is the single door into your servers, so it has to be the\\nmost locked-down machine you run.** This checklist covers the controls that\\nmatter, in the order they usually go wrong, with the OpenSSH settings for a\\nplain jump host and what a PAM gateway does for you.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## 1. One identity per person\\n\\nShared accounts (`ubuntu`, `admin`, `deploy`) destroy attribution. Every\\nperson gets their own login on the bastion, even if they land on a shared\\naccount on the target.\\n\\n- Plain bastion: one Unix user per person, provisioned from your directory.\\n- Gateway: users exist at the gateway; the target account (`remote_user`) is\\n  part of the grant. Orion Belt logs both who you are and which account you\\n  used.\\n\\n## 2. Keys only, no passwords\\n\\n```text\\n# /etc/ssh/sshd_config\\nPasswordAuthentication no\\nKbdInteractiveAuthentication no\\nPermitRootLogin no\\nPubkeyAuthentication yes\\n```\\n\\nPrefer ed25519 keys. Hardware-backed keys (`ed25519-sk`, `ecdsa-sk`) keep\\nthe private key on a FIDO2 token, so a stolen laptop disk is not a stolen key.\\n\\n## 3. Short-lived credentials beat key rotation\\n\\nLong-lived keys in `authorized_keys` outlive the people who own them.\\n[SSH certificates](https://man.openbsd.org/ssh-keygen#CERTIFICATES) signed\\nby a CA expire on their own. With OpenSSH, set `TrustedUserCAKeys` on the\\nhosts and issue certificates with `ssh-keygen -s ca -V +8h`. Orion Belt can\\nrun that CA for you ([optional SSH CA](/blog/optional-ssh-ca)).\\n\\n## 4. MFA at the door\\n\\nThe bastion is where MFA belongs: one place to enforce it, rather than every\\nhost. Options: FIDO2 SSH keys (the touch is the second factor), TOTP through\\nPAM, or a gateway that requires TOTP or WebAuthn for the console\\n([MFA and WebAuthn](/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh)).\\n\\n## 5. Expose as little as possible\\n\\n- Only the bastion accepts SSH from outside. Targets accept SSH only from the\\n  bastion\'s address, or not at all.\\n- With a reverse-agent gateway, targets open **no inbound port**: the agent\\n  dials out ([why](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)). There is nothing to scan.\\n- Disable features you don\'t use on the bastion itself:\\n\\n```text\\nAllowTcpForwarding no      # unless you rely on ProxyJump through it\\nX11Forwarding no\\nAllowAgentForwarding no\\nPermitTunnel no\\n```\\n\\nAgent forwarding on a shared bastion lets anyone with root on it use your\\nkeys while you\'re connected. Use `ProxyJump` (`ssh -J bastion target`)\\ninstead: the connection to the target is end to end from your laptop.\\n\\n## 6. Least privilege, and time-boxed\\n\\nStanding access to production is the norm and the problem. Grant access per\\nmachine, and for privileged access, for a limited time after approval.\\nIn [NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final),\\nthe controls auditors map this to are AC-6 (least privilege) and AC-2\\n(account management).\\n\\nIn Orion Belt, grants are per user, machine and remote user, and\\n[just-in-time requests](/blog/jit-access-approvals) expire on their own.\\n\\n## 7. Record sessions off the target\\n\\nLogs on the target can be edited by root on the target. Record at the\\nbastion. On a plain jump host with `ProxyJump` you can\'t see inside the\\nsession (it\'s encrypted end to end), so recording needs a gateway that\\nterminates SSH. See\\n[how to audit SSH access on Linux](/blog/how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux).\\n\\n## 8. Ship logs, alert on the unusual\\n\\nSend bastion logs off the box as they\'re written: sshd at `LogLevel VERBOSE`\\n(logs key fingerprints), the gateway\'s audit log, and metrics. Alert on\\nlogins outside working hours, new source addresses and failed MFA bursts.\\n\\n## 9. Patch it first\\n\\nThe bastion is internet-facing and holds the keys to everything. It goes\\nfirst in every OpenSSH and kernel update cycle, not last.\\n\\n## 10. Plan break-glass before you need it\\n\\nWhen the bastion or your identity provider is down, how do you get in? Write\\nit down: a sealed local admin credential, who can use it, and how its use is\\naudited and rotated afterwards.\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] One identity per person; no shared bastion logins\\n- [ ] Keys only; root login off\\n- [ ] Short-lived certificates or a gateway instead of static keys\\n- [ ] MFA enforced at the bastion\\n- [ ] Targets accept SSH only via the bastion (or open no port at all)\\n- [ ] Agent forwarding off; `ProxyJump` for hops\\n- [ ] Per-machine, time-boxed grants for privileged access\\n- [ ] Sessions recorded off the target\\n- [ ] Logs shipped off-host with alerts\\n- [ ] Bastion patched first\\n- [ ] Documented break-glass\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Is a bastion host still needed with a VPN?**\\nA VPN gives network access; it doesn\'t decide which machine a person may log\\ninto, record the session or expire access. Many teams run both.\\n\\n**ProxyJump or agent forwarding?**\\nProxyJump. Agent forwarding exposes your agent to anyone with root on the\\nbastion for as long as you\'re connected.\\n\\n**What\'s the difference between a bastion and PAM?**\\nA bastion controls the network path. PAM adds who may access what, when, with\\nwhose approval, and a record of it. More in\\n[SSH bastion vs PAM](/blog/bastion-vs-pam)."},{"id":"orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-05-orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2.md","title":"Orion Belt 1.1 and 1.2 \u2014 tracing, report exports, usage analytics and a one-line installer","description":"Orion Belt 1.1.0 adds OpenTelemetry tracing across the gateway and agent, PDF/CSV/SIEM report exports, a usage analytics dashboard, an org-wide notification policy and a Go SDK. 1.2.0 adds a one-line server installer and in-app notifications.","date":"2026-08-05T12:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"},{"inline":false,"label":"Observability","permalink":"/blog/tags/observability","description":"Metrics, logs and tracing"}],"readingTime":2.4,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2","title":"Orion Belt 1.1 and 1.2 \u2014 tracing, report exports, usage analytics and a one-line installer","description":"Orion Belt 1.1.0 adds OpenTelemetry tracing across the gateway and agent, PDF/CSV/SIEM report exports, a usage analytics dashboard, an org-wide notification policy and a Go SDK. 1.2.0 adds a one-line server installer and in-app notifications.","date":"2026-08-05T12:00:00.000Z","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","ops","observability"],"keywords":["Orion Belt 1.1","Orion Belt 1.2","SSH OpenTelemetry","PAM report export","SSH usage analytics","PAM Go SDK","SSH gateway installer"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SSH bastion host best practices \u2014 a hardening checklist","permalink":"/blog/ssh-bastion-host-best-practices"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to audit SSH access on Linux \u2014 what sshd, auditd, tlog and a gateway each record","permalink":"/blog/how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux"}},"content":"Two releases landed two weeks after 1.0: **Orion Belt 1.1.0** on July 30\\nand **1.2.0** on July 31. 1.1 is about seeing what the gateway does. 1.2 is\\nabout getting it installed.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## 1.1.0: observability and reporting\\n\\n### OpenTelemetry tracing\\n\\nThe gateway and agents can emit OTLP traces to any collector (OpenTelemetry\\nCollector, Jaeger, Tempo, Honeycomb). One trace covers an SSH session end to\\nend: authorization at the gateway, the gateway \u2192 agent hop, and the agent \u2192\\ntarget hop. When a connection is slow, the trace shows which hop. It\'s off by\\ndefault and costs nothing when disabled. Details in\\n[Tracing SSH sessions with OpenTelemetry](/blog/opentelemetry-ssh-tracing).\\n\\n### Report exports\\n\\nAudit and security reports export as **PDF** for reviewers, **CSV** for\\nspreadsheets, and a **SIEM-oriented** format for log pipelines, from the\\nconsole or the API.\\n\\n### Usage analytics\\n\\nA dashboard with access volume, approval latency and the most used targets.\\nIt answers \\"who actually uses production access, and how long do approvals\\ntake?\\" without exporting the audit log first.\\n\\n### Notification policy\\n\\nUsers choose which notifications they get; admins set an org-wide policy that\\nbounds those choices. A user can\'t opt out of something the policy requires.\\n\\n### Go SDK\\n\\nA Go client for the public API, for scripts and integrations that would\\notherwise hand-roll HTTP calls. First-pass Python, JavaScript and .NET\\nclients ship alongside it as a starting point.\\n\\n## 1.2.0: install and day-to-day\\n\\n### One-line server installer\\n\\n`scripts/install-server.sh` installs the server from the distribution\\npackages, sets up a local PostgreSQL (or reuses one, or takes a DSN), asks for\\nthe public URL and WebAuthn settings, enables the systemd or OpenRC service,\\nand writes an `osh` client config for the user who ran it.\\n\\nThe public URL is now a first-class setting (`server.public_url`). The\\n**Add agent** flow and the setup wizard use it, so generated agent install\\ncommands point at the right address.\\n\\n### In-app notifications\\n\\nAccess request events appear in the console\'s notification bell. Admins also\\nget a notice when WebAuthn is enabled in the config but fails to start, which\\nused to fail silently.\\n\\n### Fixes\\n\\n- Agents and users with recorded sessions or grants can be hard-deleted; the\\n  schema repairs its foreign keys on migrate.\\n- Deleting an agent through the API also removes its synthetic agent user,\\n  as the CLI already did.\\n\\n## Known limitations in 1.2.0\\n\\nFrom the release notes, so you can plan around them:\\n\\n- Revoking or pausing an agent can be undone when the agent reconnects.\\n- `POST /public/register/client` still accepts `is_admin` for lab bootstrap;\\n  lock it down in production.\\n- Recording retention targets cast files by modification time and may leave\\n  encrypted files or session rows behind.\\n\\n## Upgrade\\n\\nInstall 1.2.0 and restart. Migrations run on start.\\n\\nRelease notes:\\n[v1.1.0](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.1.0) \xb7\\n[v1.2.0](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/releases/tag/v1.2.0)"},{"id":"how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-29-how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux.md","title":"How to audit SSH access on Linux \u2014 what sshd, auditd, tlog and a gateway each record","description":"A practical comparison of the ways to audit SSH sessions on Linux \u2014 sshd logs, auditd, pam_tty_audit, tlog, script and an SSH gateway \u2014 with what each one captures, what it misses, and how to combine them.","date":"2026-07-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux","description":"Linux packages, distributions and host tooling"}],"readingTime":3.78,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux","title":"How to audit SSH access on Linux \u2014 what sshd, auditd, tlog and a gateway each record","description":"A practical comparison of the ways to audit SSH sessions on Linux \u2014 sshd logs, auditd, pam_tty_audit, tlog, script and an SSH gateway \u2014 with what each one captures, what it misses, and how to combine them.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","security","linux"],"keywords":["audit SSH sessions","SSH audit log","record SSH sessions Linux","auditd SSH","tlog","pam_tty_audit","SSH session recording"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt 1.1 and 1.2 \u2014 tracing, report exports, usage analytics and a one-line installer","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-v1-1-and-v1-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Orion Belt vs Teleport \u2014 a practical Teleport alternative for SSH PAM","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport"}},"content":"**To audit SSH access on Linux you need three things: who logged in, what\\nthey did, and proof that the record wasn\'t changed by the person being\\naudited.** No single host tool gives you all three. This guide compares the\\ncommon options and shows where a gateway like Orion Belt fits.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The short answer\\n\\n| Tool | Who logged in | What they typed | What they saw | Survives root on the host |\\n| --- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\n| `sshd` logs (`LogLevel VERBOSE`) | Yes, with key fingerprint | No | No | No |\\n| `auditd` (execve rules) | Yes (auid) | Commands run, not keystrokes | No | Only if shipped off-host at once |\\n| `pam_tty_audit` | Yes | Keystrokes | No | Only if shipped off-host at once |\\n| `tlog` | Yes | Yes | Yes | Only if shipped off-host at once |\\n| `script` / shell history | Self-reported | Partly | `script` only | No |\\n| SSH gateway recording | Yes | Yes | Yes | **Yes**: recorded before the host |\\n\\nThe last column is the one auditors care about. Anything recorded on the\\ntarget can be edited by whoever has root on the target, and the people you\\nmost need to audit usually do.\\n\\n## sshd logs: the baseline\\n\\nSet `LogLevel VERBOSE` in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`. sshd then logs the\\nfingerprint of the key used for each login, not only the username, so you\\ncan tell which of a shared account\'s keys was used\\n([sshd_config(5)](https://man.openbsd.org/sshd_config#LogLevel)).\\n\\nIt tells you nothing about what happened after login.\\n\\n## auditd: commands, not sessions\\n\\nThe Linux audit framework can log every `execve` call with the login UID\\n(`auid`), which survives `sudo`:\\n\\n```bash\\n-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k user-commands\\n```\\n\\nYou get a reliable list of programs run and their arguments. You do not get\\nkeystrokes inside interactive programs (`vim`, `psql`, `mysql`) or anything\\nthe user saw on screen. Volume is high; plan for log shipping.\\n\\n## pam_tty_audit: keystrokes\\n\\n`pam_tty_audit` records TTY input into the audit log\\n([pam_tty_audit(8)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/pam_tty_audit.8.html)).\\nIt catches keystrokes in interactive programs. By default it skips input\\ntyped with echo off, such as passwords; the `log_passwd` option records that\\ntoo. Output is not recorded, so a reviewer sees what was typed but not what the system\\nanswered.\\n\\n## tlog: full terminal recording on the host\\n\\n[tlog](https://github.com/Scribery/tlog) records terminal input and output\\nas JSON and can replay it. It\'s the most complete host-side option and is\\npackaged on RHEL and Fedora. Like everything on this list so far, it runs on\\nthe target, under the target\'s root.\\n\\n## script and shell history: not evidence\\n\\n`script` and `~/.bash_history` are useful for the user. They are written by\\nthe user, can be turned off by the user, and prove nothing.\\n\\n## Recording at the gateway\\n\\nAn SSH gateway sits between the user and the host and records the session\\nstream as it passes through. The target never holds the recording, so root on\\nthe target can\'t alter it.\\n\\nIn Orion Belt the gateway records every SSH session, optionally compressed\\nand encrypted at rest with a retention period. Admins can\\n[watch a session live](/blog/live-session-watch) and replay it in the\\nconsole. Each session is tied to the user\'s identity at the gateway, not to\\nthe shared Unix account on the host, and to the approval that allowed it if\\naccess was [requested just in time](/blog/jit-access-approvals).\\n\\nTargets also don\'t need an inbound SSH port: the\\n[agent dials out](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents) to the gateway.\\n\\n## A layered setup that holds up\\n\\n1. **Gateway recording** for every interactive session: the tamper-resistant\\n   record of who did what.\\n2. **auditd** on hosts, shipped off-host, for commands run by services,\\n   cron jobs and anything that didn\'t come through the gateway.\\n3. **sshd `LogLevel VERBOSE`**, to spot direct logins that bypassed the\\n   gateway. Once the gateway is in place, there shouldn\'t be any.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Can I record SSH sessions without installing anything on the server?**\\nYes, at a gateway. Host-side tools (tlog, pam_tty_audit) need packages and\\nconfiguration on every server.\\n\\n**Does auditd record what a user typed in vim or psql?**\\nNo. auditd records the programs started (`execve`). Keystrokes need\\n`pam_tty_audit` or full session recording.\\n\\n**Where should SSH audit logs be stored?**\\nOff the host, as soon as they\'re written, somewhere the audited users can\'t\\nwrite to. A gateway does this by design.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Why session recording is hard](/blog/session-recording-is-hard)\\n- [SSH bastion vs PAM](/blog/bastion-vs-pam)\\n- [Try Orion Belt in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes)"},{"id":"orion-belt-vs-teleport","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-16-orion-belt-vs-teleport.md","title":"Orion Belt vs Teleport \u2014 a practical Teleport alternative for SSH PAM","description":"Compare Orion Belt and Teleport for self-hosted privileged access. When a lighter SSH bastion/PAM gateway wins, when Teleport\u2019s broader platform is worth it, plus Boundary and classic bastions.","date":"2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Teleport","permalink":"/blog/tags/teleport","description":"Comparisons and alternatives"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"}],"readingTime":3.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"orion-belt-vs-teleport","title":"Orion Belt vs Teleport \u2014 a practical Teleport alternative for SSH PAM","description":"Compare Orion Belt and Teleport for self-hosted privileged access. When a lighter SSH bastion/PAM gateway wins, when Teleport\u2019s broader platform is worth it, plus Boundary and classic bastions.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","pam","teleport","self-hosted"],"keywords":["Teleport alternative","Orion Belt vs Teleport","open source PAM","self-hosted SSH bastion","Boundary vs bastion"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to audit SSH access on Linux \u2014 what sshd, auditd, tlog and a gateway each record","permalink":"/blog/how-to-audit-ssh-access-linux"},"nextItem":{"title":"Orion Belt v1.0 \u2014 self-hosted SSH PAM gateway is stable","permalink":"/blog/welcome-orion-belt-v1"}},"content":"**Short version:** Orion Belt is a self-hosted **SSH access gateway with PAM workflows**. Teleport is a **broad zero-trust access platform**. If you mainly need mediated SSH, recording, JIT, and MFA \u2014 without inbound ports on every host or a large ops surface \u2014 Orion Belt is the lighter **Teleport alternative**.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis comparison is for practitioners evaluating **open source PAM** / **self-hosted privileged access** options for SSH, not a dunk contest. Licensing differs; verify current Teleport terms for your use case. Orion Belt is open source under the AGPL v3.0 since 1.5.0 ([details](/blog/orion-belt-agpl)).\\n\\n## Who should read this\\n\\n- Teams searching for a **Teleport alternative** focused on SSH  \\n- Homelab and SMB operators who find Teleport\u2019s surface area large  \\n- Security engineers comparing bastion vs platform approaches  \\n\\n## Side-by-side\\n\\n| Dimension | Orion Belt | Teleport | HashiCorp Boundary | Traditional bastion |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Primary job | SSH PAM / bastion gateway | Multi-protocol zero-trust access | Identity-aware session brokering | Network jump point |\\n| Protocols (typical) | SSH / SCP (focused) | SSH, K8s, DB, web, Windows, \u2026 | Many via targets/workers | Whatever you tunnel |\\n| How targets are reached | **Agents dial out** (reverse SSH) | Node agents / tunnels / IoT | Workers / proxies | Inbound to bastion; often inbound to hosts |\\n| Inbound on targets | Not required for agent path | Depends on mode | Depends on layout | Common |\\n| Session recording | Yes (+ live watch) | Yes | Yes (with recording path) | DIY |\\n| JIT / approvals | Built-in (+ ChatOps) | Access requests / roles | Via IdP / workflows | Rare |\\n| MFA | TOTP + WebAuthn; FIDO SSH keys | Strong IdP / MFA story | Via IdP | DIY |\\n| Authorization | ReBAC (+ optional OpenFGA) | RBAC / roles / traits | IAM-centric | OS users / keys |\\n| SSH CA | Optional built-in | First-class cert story | Different model | Manual / none |\\n| Ops weight | Smaller SSH-focused deploy | Larger platform | Medium\u2013large | Small but feature-poor |\\n| Hosting | Self-hosted | Self-hosted or cloud | Self-hosted or HCP | Self-hosted |\\n\\n## Architecture contrast\\n\\n### Traditional bastion\\n\\n```text\\nOperator \u2192 (SSH) \u2192 Bastion \u2192 (SSH) \u2192 Target\\n```\\n\\nYou still manage keys, often lack recording/JIT, and frequently still expose SSH somewhere on the path.\\n\\n### Orion Belt\\n\\n```text\\nOperator \u2192 Gateway \u2190 (reverse SSH) \u2190 Agent on target\\n```\\n\\nThe target initiates the tunnel. The gateway brokers sessions, enforces policy, and can record or live-watch. Background: [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents).\\n\\n### Teleport-shaped platforms\\n\\nPowerful and broad: many protocols, deep identity integration, large feature surface \u2014 and correspondingly more to deploy, learn, and operate.\\n\\n## When to choose Orion Belt\\n\\nChoose Orion Belt if most of these are true:\\n\\n- Privileged access is **mostly SSH**  \\n- You want **no inbound SSH** on application hosts  \\n- You want **recording + live watch + JIT** without a PAM appliance  \\n- You prefer **self-hosted**, Linux-first, inspectable code  \\n- You accept a **narrower scope** today (OIDC, HA, other protocols on the roadmap)\\n\\nHands-on: [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes).\\n\\n## When Teleport (or similar) is a better fit\\n\\nChoose Teleport if you need:\\n\\n- First-class **Kubernetes / DB / Windows / web** access in one product now  \\n- A large ecosystem and org-wide zero-trust program already centered on that tool  \\n- Features Orion Belt has not shipped yet  \\n\\n## When Boundary fits better\\n\\nBoundary shines when the center of gravity is **identity + credential brokering** in a HashiCorp world \u2014 not \u201CSSH bastion with PAM workflows\u201D as the primary UX.\\n\\n## Security tradeoffs (reverse agents)\\n\\n**Gain:** targets need outbound reachability to the gateway, not inbound SSH from attackers\u2019 scan range.  \\n**Cost:** offline agents are unreachable; the gateway is a high-value control plane \u2014 harden it, encrypt recordings, require MFA for admins.\\n\\n## SEO-style FAQ\\n\\n**Is Orion Belt a Teleport alternative?**  \\nFor SSH-centric self-hosted PAM/bastion needs, yes. For full multi-protocol zero trust, Teleport remains broader.\\n\\n**Is Orion Belt open source?**  \\nYes, since 1.5.0: GNU AGPL v3.0 only, an OSI-approved license. Free to use, self-host and run commercially; if you distribute it or offer a modified version over a network, you publish your source under the same license. Releases 1.0 to 1.4 were Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause.\\n\\n**Can I migrate from a jump host?**  \\nYes: enroll agents, move humans to `user+host@gateway`, turn on recording, then tighten inbound SSH. See [bastion vs PAM](/blog/bastion-vs-pam).\\n\\n## Try before you debate\\n\\n```bash\\ngit clone https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt.git\\ncd orion-belt\\n./scripts/docker-quickstart.sh\\n```\\n\\n[Documentation](/docs/introduction) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt) \xb7 [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ)"},{"id":"welcome-orion-belt-v1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/welcome-orion-belt-v1","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-16-welcome-orion-belt-v1.md","title":"Orion Belt v1.0 \u2014 self-hosted SSH PAM gateway is stable","description":"Orion Belt v1.0 is released \u2014 self-hosted SSH access gateway with reverse agents, session recording, JIT, MFA, and ReBAC. Try the 10-minute path and join early operators.","date":"2026-07-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"}],"readingTime":1.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome-orion-belt-v1","title":"Orion Belt v1.0 \u2014 self-hosted SSH PAM gateway is stable","description":"Orion Belt v1.0 is released \u2014 self-hosted SSH access gateway with reverse agents, session recording, JIT, MFA, and ReBAC. Try the 10-minute path and join early operators.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","pam","self-hosted"],"keywords":["Orion Belt v1","self-hosted PAM","SSH bastion release","Teleport alternative"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt vs Teleport \u2014 a practical Teleport alternative for SSH PAM","permalink":"/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport"},"nextItem":{"title":"Join Orion Belt early operators \u2014 first self-hosted SSH PAM deployments","permalink":"/blog/looking-for-early-operators"}},"content":"**Orion Belt v1.0** is out: a stable, self-hosted **SSH access gateway with PAM workflows**. Reverse agents, session recording, live watch, JIT approvals, MFA/WebAuthn, ReBAC, optional SSH CA, Linux packages, and a web console \u2014 without a SaaS dependency.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis is the release note for operators who have been waiting for a version line we are willing to call production-shaped for the SSH slice.\\n\\n## What \u201Cv1.0\u201D means here\\n\\n- SSH proxy / bastion path we will not casually break  \\n- Documented Docker quickstart and package installs  \\n- Recording + live watch on the gateway path  \\n- JIT + ChatOps approvals  \\n- MFA options for the console  \\n- ReBAC (optional OpenFGA)  \\n- Optional SSH CA  \\n- OpenAPI + observability docs in the main repo  \\n\\nStill on the roadmap (not pretend-done): OIDC, HA, additional protocols. Honesty > checkbox theater \u2014 [lessons](/blog/lessons-building-self-hosted-pam).\\n\\n## Positioning\\n\\nTeleport-like SSH access control \u2014 **without** opening inbound ports on every host or adopting a large platform.\\n\\nCompare: [Orion Belt vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport). Landscape: [open-source PAM guide](/blog/open-source-pam-landscape).\\n\\n## Try it in about ten minutes\\n\\n```bash\\ngit clone https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt.git\\ncd orion-belt\\n./scripts/docker-quickstart.sh\\n```\\n\\nThen: [Try Orion Belt in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes) through agent \u2192 SSH \u2192 Sessions playback.\\n\\n## License\\n\\n:::info[Update, November 2026]\\nSince 1.5.0, Orion Belt is open source under the GNU AGPL v3.0. See\\n[Orion Belt is now AGPL v3.0](/blog/orion-belt-agpl).\\n:::\\n\\nAt 1.0: Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause \u2014 free to self-host and use internally; you cannot sell Orion Belt as a product or substantially derived hosted service. [Plain-language FAQ](/blog/commons-clause-honesty).\\n\\n## Join early operators\\n\\nWe want deployments and sharp feedback more than vanity metrics. [Early operators call](/blog/looking-for-early-operators) \xb7 [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ) \xb7 [Discussions](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/discussions)\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- [orion-belt.dev](https://orion-belt.dev)  \\n- [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)  \\n- [Documentation](/docs/introduction)"},{"id":"looking-for-early-operators","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/looking-for-early-operators","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-08-looking-for-early-operators.md","title":"Join Orion Belt early operators \u2014 first self-hosted SSH PAM deployments","description":"We are looking for the first ~20 teams and labs running Orion Belt v1.0. Get install help, influence the roadmap, and help shape a self-hosted Teleport alternative for SSH.","date":"2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"}],"readingTime":1.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"looking-for-early-operators","title":"Join Orion Belt early operators \u2014 first self-hosted SSH PAM deployments","description":"We are looking for the first ~20 teams and labs running Orion Belt v1.0. Get install help, influence the roadmap, and help shape a self-hosted Teleport alternative for SSH.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["self-hosted","ops","pam"],"keywords":["self-hosted PAM community","SSH bastion early access","Orion Belt operators"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Orion Belt v1.0 \u2014 self-hosted SSH PAM gateway is stable","permalink":"/blog/welcome-orion-belt-v1"},"nextItem":{"title":"Lessons learned building a self-hosted SSH PAM gateway","permalink":"/blog/lessons-building-self-hosted-pam"}},"content":"GitHub stars are a lagging indicator. The leading indicator we care about is simple: **are serious people deploying Orion Belt and telling us what breaks?**\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis is an open call for **early operators** \u2014 roughly the first twenty labs or small teams running v1.0 as a real **self-hosted SSH PAM / bastion** path.\\n\\n## Who we want\\n\\n- Homelab / self-hosting users with more than one Linux box  \\n- Small company SRE/DevOps teams needing recording + JIT without SaaS  \\n- Security engineers evaluating a **Teleport alternative** for SSH-only scope  \\n- People willing to file reproducible bugs (Docker quickstart welcome)\\n\\n## Who we are not optimizing for (yet)\\n\\n- Enterprises needing HA + OIDC + multi-protocol on day one  \\n- Teams whose policy rules out AGPL software ([license notes](/blog/orion-belt-agpl); Orion Belt moved from Commons Clause to the AGPL v3.0 in 1.5.0)  \\n- Drive-by \u201Cplease add my niche protocol\u201D without deploying SSH first  \\n\\n## What you get\\n\\n- Direct help on [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ) when agents will not dial out  \\n- Faster eyes on your issues and PRs  \\n- Input on roadmap sequencing (OIDC, HA, etc.)  \\n- Optional public credit as an early operator  \\n\\n## What we ask\\n\\n1. Complete [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes)  \\n2. Leave it running on something you actually use (even a homelab VM)  \\n3. Open GitHub issues with versions, logs, and repro steps  \\n4. Tell us if docs lied  \\n\\n## How to raise your hand\\n\\n- Message in Discord  \\n- Open a [GitHub Discussion](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/discussions)  \\n- Or comment on an issue that you are running v1.0 in anger  \\n\\nMetrics we track privately for the first month: install attempts, active deployments, external issues \u2014 not vanity follower counts. Background: [lessons building PAM](/blog/lessons-building-self-hosted-pam).\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [v1.0 announcement](/blog/welcome-orion-belt-v1)  \\n- [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)"},{"id":"lessons-building-self-hosted-pam","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/lessons-building-self-hosted-pam","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-26-lessons-building-self-hosted-pam.md","title":"Lessons learned building a self-hosted SSH PAM gateway","description":"Practical lessons from building Orion Belt \u2014 access control vs audit, UX for JIT, reverse tunnel tradeoffs, licensing honesty, and keeping a 10-minute install path sacred.","date":"2026-06-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture","description":"Design and tradeoffs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":1.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"lessons-building-self-hosted-pam","title":"Lessons learned building a self-hosted SSH PAM gateway","description":"Practical lessons from building Orion Belt \u2014 access control vs audit, UX for JIT, reverse tunnel tradeoffs, licensing honesty, and keeping a 10-minute install path sacred.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","architecture","ops"],"keywords":["self-hosted PAM","building SSH bastion","privileged access lessons","open source PAM"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Join Orion Belt early operators \u2014 first self-hosted SSH PAM deployments","permalink":"/blog/looking-for-early-operators"},"nextItem":{"title":"Shipping deb/rpm/apk packages and a Docker lab for SSH PAM","permalink":"/blog/linux-packages-and-labs"}},"content":"Orion Belt v1.0 is our line in the sand for a stable **self-hosted SSH PAM** slice. These are the lessons that shaped it \u2014 useful if you are building similar infrastructure or evaluating whether our tradeoffs match yours.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## 1. Access control is easy compared to audit\\n\\nReBAC graphs are approachable. Faithful, replayable, retainable **session recordings** are not. Budget engineering time accordingly. Deep dive: [Session recording is hard](/blog/session-recording-is-hard).\\n\\n## 2. UX beats feature count for JIT\\n\\nIf temporary access takes longer than sharing a key, standing keys win. ChatOps approvals and a short CLI/UI path matter more than a twelfth policy DSL. See [JIT access](/blog/jit-access-approvals).\\n\\n## 3. Reverse tunnels move risk; they don\u2019t delete it\\n\\nAgents dialing out remove inbound SSH on targets and concentrate trust on the gateway. Harden the gateway like a directory service. Explained in [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents).\\n\\n## 4. Optional complexity must stay optional\\n\\nSSH CA, OpenFGA, and WebAuthn are valuable. None of them should block the first recorded session. Optional flags exist so labs stay fast ([SSH CA](/blog/optional-ssh-ca), [MFA](/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh)).\\n\\n## 5. License clarity is a security feature\\n\\nPeople evaluating PAM software are professionally paranoid. Commons Clause disclosed early builds trust even when it loses some adopters. [License FAQ](/blog/commons-clause-honesty).\\n\\nUpdate, November 2026: in 1.5.0 we moved to the AGPL v3.0, an OSI-approved license. [Why](/blog/orion-belt-agpl).\\n\\n## 6. Protect the 10-minute path like an API\\n\\nEvery feature PR should ask: did we break `docker-quickstart` \u2192 agent \u2192 Sessions? If yes, fix or document before merge. [Install path design](/blog/ten-minute-install-path).\\n\\n## 7. Say no to protocol sprawl in v1\\n\\nKubernetes and database proxies are real needs. Shipping a mediocre version of each would dilute the SSH promise. Roadmap honesty > checkbox theater. Comparison mindset: [vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport).\\n\\n## 8. Dual packaging is worth it\\n\\nDocker converts; packages retain. [Packages and labs](/blog/linux-packages-and-labs).\\n\\n## What we want next from the community\\n\\nSharp issues from people who **deployed** something \u2014 not drive-by feature wishes. If that is you: [early operators](/blog/looking-for-early-operators).\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Announcing Orion Belt](/blog/announcing-orion-belt)  \\n- [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)"},{"id":"linux-packages-and-labs","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/linux-packages-and-labs","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-12-linux-packages-and-labs.md","title":"Shipping deb/rpm/apk packages and a Docker lab for SSH PAM","description":"Why Orion Belt ships native Linux packages and a Docker quickstart \u2014 two audiences for self-hosted SSH bastion installs, signed repos, and reproducible bug reports.","date":"2026-06-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/blog/tags/linux","description":"Linux packages, distributions and host tooling"}],"readingTime":1.66,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"linux-packages-and-labs","title":"Shipping deb/rpm/apk packages and a Docker lab for SSH PAM","description":"Why Orion Belt ships native Linux packages and a Docker quickstart \u2014 two audiences for self-hosted SSH bastion installs, signed repos, and reproducible bug reports.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ops","self-hosted","linux"],"keywords":["SSH bastion packages","self-hosted PAM Docker","deb rpm apk","Linux privileged access"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Lessons learned building a self-hosted SSH PAM gateway","permalink":"/blog/lessons-building-self-hosted-pam"},"nextItem":{"title":"SSH bastion host vs PAM gateway \u2014 which problem are you solving?","permalink":"/blog/bastion-vs-pam"}},"content":"Real gateways want **systemd** and signed package repos. First-time evaluators want `docker compose` and a browser. Orion Belt ships both on purpose. This post explains the split for operators installing a **self-hosted SSH bastion / PAM** stack.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Two audiences, two artifacts\\n\\n| Audience | Success looks like | Artifact |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Security / platform eng | Survives reboot, upgrades, SELinux | deb / rpm / apk + docs |\\n| Curious SRE on a laptop | Recorded session in 10 minutes | `docker-quickstart.sh` |\\n\\nIf you only ship packages, blog traffic bounces. If you only ship Docker, production teams bounce. Document them as **siblings**, not one confused path \u2014 see [10-minute install path](/blog/ten-minute-install-path).\\n\\n## What \u201Cmake packages\u201D is for\\n\\nFrom the product repo:\\n\\n```bash\\nmake packages\\n# optional signed repos with ORION_REQUIRE_SIGN=1\\n```\\n\\nYou get installable **server**, **agent**, and **tools** packages for common Linux distros. First-run after packages: server YAML, `setup`, Add agent \u2014 covered in [Server setup](/docs/quickstart/server) and the main repo `SETUP.md` / `PACKAGING.md`.\\n\\n## What Docker is for\\n\\n- Marketing conversion  \\n- Reproducing GitHub issues  \\n- Homelabs and CI smoke tests  \\n- Demoing reverse agents without cloud VMs  \\n\\nWhen someone says \u201Cagent won\u2019t connect,\u201D asking for a Docker quickstart repro saves days of \u201Cwhat\u2019s in your custom unit file?\u201D\\n\\n## Signed repos and supply chain\\n\\nGPG-signed APT/RPM trees matter once you leave the lab. Treat packaging keys like release keys: generate, backup, publish the public key, and document fingerprint verification. The scripts in-tree (`gen-packaging-key.sh`, `publish-repos.sh`) exist so you are not inventing that ceremony under pressure.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Can I run production on Docker Compose?**  \\nYou can. Many teams still prefer packages + systemd for host networking and upgrade habits. Compose is not immoral; just own backup/restore for volumes.\\n\\n**Do agents need the same package format as the server?**  \\nMatch the target OS. The UI install script path is built around that.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes)  \\n- [Lessons building PAM](/blog/lessons-building-self-hosted-pam)  \\n- [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)"},{"id":"bastion-vs-pam","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/bastion-vs-pam","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-29-bastion-vs-pam.md","title":"SSH bastion host vs PAM gateway \u2014 which problem are you solving?","description":"Clarify bastion hosts versus privileged access management (PAM) for SSH. When a jump box is enough, when you need recording and JIT, and where Orion Belt fits.","date":"2026-05-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture","description":"Design and tradeoffs"}],"readingTime":1.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"bastion-vs-pam","title":"SSH bastion host vs PAM gateway \u2014 which problem are you solving?","description":"Clarify bastion hosts versus privileged access management (PAM) for SSH. When a jump box is enough, when you need recording and JIT, and where Orion Belt fits.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","pam","architecture"],"keywords":["SSH bastion host","PAM vs bastion","privileged access management SSH","self-hosted bastion"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Shipping deb/rpm/apk packages and a Docker lab for SSH PAM","permalink":"/blog/linux-packages-and-labs"},"nextItem":{"title":"Live SSH session watch for self-hosted PAM gateways","permalink":"/blog/live-session-watch"}},"content":"Engineers say \u201Cbastion\u201D to mean three different products. That ambiguity wastes evaluation time. This article separates **SSH bastion hosts** from **PAM gateways**, and places Orion Belt on that map.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Definitions that help\\n\\n### Jump host / classic bastion\\n\\nA hardened server you SSH into, then pivot to internal hosts. Network rendezvous. Policy and audit are usually DIY.\\n\\n### SSH access gateway / recording bastion\\n\\nA control plane that brokers SSH, often with session recording and centralized authn. May use reverse tunnels or agents.\\n\\n### Privileged access management (PAM)\\n\\nA broader discipline: joiner-mover-leaver, credential vaults, password rotation, multi-protocol privileged sessions, tickets, and compliance reports. SSH is one slice.\\n\\n## Decision table\\n\\n| Need | Jump host | SSH gateway | Full PAM suite |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Reach internal SSH | \u2713 | \u2713 | \u2713 |\\n| Per-machine grants | \u2717 / DIY | \u2713 | \u2713 |\\n| Session recording | \u2717 / DIY | \u2713 | \u2713 |\\n| JIT approvals | \u2717 | \u2713 / varies | \u2713 |\\n| K8s/DB/Windows too | \u2717 | \u2717 / limited | \u2713 |\\n| Lowest ops cost | \u2713 | Medium | High |\\n\\n## Where Orion Belt fits\\n\\nOrion Belt is a **self-hosted SSH PAM gateway** (bastion + PAM workflows for SSH):\\n\\n- Reverse agents (dial out)  \\n- Recording + live watch  \\n- JIT + MFA + ReBAC  \\n- Not a full multi-protocol PAM suite in v1  \\n\\nIf your RFP says \u201CPAM\u201D but the checklist is SSH + audit + approvals, a focused gateway may close the RFP without a platform migration. If the checklist includes password checkout for Windows and database proxying next quarter, shortlist broader tools \u2014 see [open-source PAM landscape](/blog/open-source-pam-landscape) and [vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport).\\n\\n## Migration pattern we see\\n\\n1. Today: jump host + VPN  \\n2. Pain: audits, shared keys, inbound SSH  \\n3. Next: SSH gateway with recording (Orion Belt class)  \\n4. Later: expand protocols or IdP integration as needed (OIDC on our roadmap)\\n\\nDo not buy step 4 on day one unless step 4\u2019s problems are already on fire.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)  \\n- [Announcing Orion Belt](/blog/announcing-orion-belt)"},{"id":"live-session-watch","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/live-session-watch","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-14-live-session-watch.md","title":"Live SSH session watch for self-hosted PAM gateways","description":"How live session watch works on an SSH bastion, privacy and permission concerns, and how Orion Belt lets admins observe active privileged sessions safely.","date":"2026-05-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"}],"readingTime":1.6,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"live-session-watch","title":"Live SSH session watch for self-hosted PAM gateways","description":"How live session watch works on an SSH bastion, privacy and permission concerns, and how Orion Belt lets admins observe active privileged sessions safely.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","security","ops"],"keywords":["live SSH session","session monitoring","privileged session watch","SSH bastion audit"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SSH bastion host vs PAM gateway \u2014 which problem are you solving?","permalink":"/blog/bastion-vs-pam"},"nextItem":{"title":"Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause \u2014 what Orion Belt\u2019s license meant before 1.5.0","permalink":"/blog/commons-clause-honesty"}},"content":"Sometimes playback tomorrow is too late. **Live session watch** lets an authorized admin observe an active SSH (or web terminal) session as it happens \u2014 useful for incident response, mentoring, and verifying break-glass work.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nUsed badly, it becomes silent surveillance. Used well, it is a sharp tool in a **self-hosted PAM** gateway. Here is how we think about it in Orion Belt.\\n\\n## Recording vs live watch\\n\\n| | Session recording | Live watch |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Timing | Persisted cast for later | Streaming view of an active session |\\n| Primary use | Compliance, forensics | Real-time oversight |\\n| Failure mode | Disk secrets / retention | Privacy / overreach |\\n\\nBoth sit on the **gateway path**. Direct-to-host SSH remains invisible. Cultural rule still applies: [session recording is hard](/blog/session-recording-is-hard).\\n\\n## Product requirements we care about\\n\\n1. **Explicit action** \u2014 an admin opens watch; it is not ambient for every operator  \\n2. **Authorization** \u2014 not every user role can watch every session  \\n3. **Honest lifecycle** \u2014 when the session ends, watch ends  \\n4. **Same trust boundary** as recording \u2014 if you can watch, you are already in the sensitive-data club  \\n\\n## When to use it\\n\\n- On-call verifying a risky production change  \\n- Approver stays online during a JIT window ([JIT access](/blog/jit-access-approvals))  \\n- Training: senior watches junior on a staging host  \\n- Suspected account misuse (with clear policy)\\n\\n## When not to use it\\n\\n- Routine browsing of everyone\u2019s terminals \u201Cbecause we can\u201D  \\n- Legal environments without disclosed monitoring policy  \\n- As a substitute for proper pairing tools when the goal is collaboration, not control  \\n\\n## Operator tips\\n\\n- Prefer watching **JIT sessions** you already approved  \\n- Prefer staging for training watches  \\n- Assume anything typed can be seen \u2014 same as recording  \\n- Combine with ChatOps approvals so the human story is in one timeline  \\n\\nTry it after a lab SSH in [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes) \u2014 open **Sessions** while the session is still active.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Sessions guide](/docs/guides/sessions)  \\n- [Security FAQ](/docs/faq/security)"},{"id":"commons-clause-honesty","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/commons-clause-honesty","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-30-commons-clause-honesty.md","title":"Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause \u2014 what Orion Belt\u2019s license meant before 1.5.0","description":"Orion Belt 1.0 to 1.4 shipped under Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause; 1.5.0 moved to the AGPL v3.0. Plain-language explanation of the old license for self-hosted PAM \u2014 what you can do, what you cannot sell, and why we disclose it on HN and Reddit.","date":"2026-04-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"}],"readingTime":2.06,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"commons-clause-honesty","title":"Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause \u2014 what Orion Belt\u2019s license meant before 1.5.0","description":"Orion Belt 1.0 to 1.4 shipped under Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause; 1.5.0 moved to the AGPL v3.0. Plain-language explanation of the old license for self-hosted PAM \u2014 what you can do, what you cannot sell, and why we disclose it on HN and Reddit.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["self-hosted","pam"],"keywords":["Commons Clause","Apache 2.0 PAM","open source license self-hosted","Orion Belt license"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Live SSH session watch for self-hosted PAM gateways","permalink":"/blog/live-session-watch"},"nextItem":{"title":"Optional SSH certificate authority for self-hosted bastions","permalink":"/blog/optional-ssh-ca"}},"content":":::info[Update, November 2026]\\nThis post describes the license of Orion Belt 1.0 to 1.4. Since 1.5.0, Orion Belt is open source under the GNU AGPL v3.0. See\\n[Orion Belt is now AGPL v3.0](/blog/orion-belt-agpl).\\n:::\\n\\nSecurity infrastructure requires trust. Burying a restrictive clause until after launch is how open-source communities learn to ignore you. Orion Belt uses **Apache License 2.0 with the Commons Clause**, and we say so in the README, website, and launch posts.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis article is the plain-language license FAQ for people evaluating Orion Belt as **self-hosted privileged access** software.\\n\\n## What you can do\\n\\nUnder Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause (as we ship it):\\n\\n- Use Orion Belt internally, including for commercial companies  \\n- Modify the code  \\n- Self-host for your team / customers\u2019 *infrastructure you operate as part of your own business* (you are not selling Orion as the product)  \\n- Read and audit the source  \\n\\n## What you cannot do\\n\\nThe Commons Clause withholds the right to **Sell** the software. In practice for Orion Belt:\\n\\n- You may not sell Orion Belt as a product  \\n- You may not offer a hosted service whose value derives substantially from Orion Belt as the product you are selling  \\n\\nIf you want to build a managed Orion SaaS as your business, that is exactly what the clause is designed to stop without permission.\\n\\n## Is it \u201COpen Source\u201D?\\n\\n**OSI \u201COpen Source\u201D** has a specific meaning. Commons Clause software is often called **source-available**. We do not pretend otherwise on Hacker News, Lobsters, or Reddit.\\n\\nIf your organization requires OSI-approved licenses only, Orion Belt may be disallowed \u2014 better to know before you invest evaluation time.\\n\\n## Why not AGPL or a pure Apache 2.0?\\n\\nDifferent projects pick different weapons. Our goals:\\n\\n1. Keep the code auditable for security buyers  \\n2. Allow free self-hosting for real users  \\n3. Prevent a cloud vendor from wrapping the repo as their PAM SaaS without a deal  \\n\\nWhether you agree with that tradeoff is personal. We would rather be declined honestly than adopted under false premises.\\n\\n## How we talk about it in public\\n\\nLaunch checklist for maintainers:\\n\\n- One sentence in Show HN / Reddit  \\n- Link to LICENSE  \\n- No \u201Cfully open source\u201D claims without qualification  \\n\\nFull legal text: [LICENSE](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/blob/master/LICENSE) \xb7 [commonsclause.com](https://commonsclause.com/)\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Orion Belt is now AGPL v3.0](/blog/orion-belt-agpl)  \\n- [Announcing Orion Belt](/blog/announcing-orion-belt)  \\n- [Open-source PAM landscape](/blog/open-source-pam-landscape)"},{"id":"optional-ssh-ca","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/optional-ssh-ca","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-15-optional-ssh-ca.md","title":"Optional SSH certificate authority for self-hosted bastions","description":"When to enable an SSH CA in a PAM gateway, how short-lived certificates reduce key sprawl, and why Orion Belt keeps SSH CA optional for labs.","date":"2026-04-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture","description":"Design and tradeoffs"}],"readingTime":1.69,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"optional-ssh-ca","title":"Optional SSH certificate authority for self-hosted bastions","description":"When to enable an SSH CA in a PAM gateway, how short-lived certificates reduce key sprawl, and why Orion Belt keeps SSH CA optional for labs.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","security","architecture"],"keywords":["SSH CA","SSH certificates","self-hosted bastion","privileged access certificates"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause \u2014 what Orion Belt\u2019s license meant before 1.5.0","permalink":"/blog/commons-clause-honesty"},"nextItem":{"title":"MFA and WebAuthn for a self-hosted SSH PAM console","permalink":"/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh"}},"content":"Long-lived `authorized_keys` entries are how SSH access rots. An **SSH certificate authority (CA)** issues short-lived user and host certificates instead. Powerful \u2014 and easy to force on users before they have a working bastion.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nOrion Belt treats SSH CA as **optional**. You can run reverse agents, recording, JIT, and MFA without it. This article explains when to turn CA on for a **self-hosted SSH PAM** deployment.\\n\\n## What an SSH CA changes\\n\\n| Without CA | With CA |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Standing user keys on many systems | Short-lived user certs from the gateway |\\n| TOFU / known_hosts drift | Host certificates for gateway/agents |\\n| Revocation by hunting files | Wait for expiry (+ revoke CA-side as designed) |\\n\\nOpenSSH has supported certificates for years; PAM/bastion products wrap the operational bits humans forget.\\n\\n## Why optional?\\n\\nBecause the **10-minute path** should not require understanding certificate principals, `AuthorizedPrincipalsFile`, and CA key ceremonies. Labs need wow-first. CA is a production maturity step \u2014 see [Designing a 10-minute install path](/blog/ten-minute-install-path).\\n\\n## When you should enable it\\n\\nEnable SSH CA when:\\n\\n- Key inventory is already painful  \\n- Contractors come and go weekly  \\n- You want host identity pinned via Host CA  \\n- Someone on the team can own CA key backup / rotation  \\n\\nKeep it off when:\\n\\n- You are still validating reverse agents and recording  \\n- Nobody owns secret management for the CA master key  \\n\\nOrion encrypts CA material at rest when enabled (`ssh_ca.master_key` style config in the main repo). Do not use example secrets.\\n\\n## Operational checklist\\n\\n1. Backup the CA master key like a root of trust  \\n2. Set host principals to the DNS/IPs clients actually use  \\n3. Document how agents receive host certs on enrollment  \\n4. Monitor expiry and clock skew (certs are time-sensitive)  \\n5. Pair with recording \u2014 certificates are not an audit trail  \\n\\nFull reference: [SSH_CA.md](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/blob/master/docs/SSH_CA.md) in the product repo.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [MFA and WebAuthn](/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh)  \\n- [ReBAC for SSH machines](/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines)  \\n- [Security best practices](/docs/guides/security)"},{"id":"mfa-webauthn-ssh","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-28-mfa-webauthn-ssh.md","title":"MFA and WebAuthn for a self-hosted SSH PAM console","description":"How Orion Belt splits console MFA (TOTP, WebAuthn) from SSH public keys and FIDO sk- keys \u2014 configuration pitfalls, rp_id origins, and practical hardening.","date":"2026-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"}],"readingTime":1.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mfa-webauthn-ssh","title":"MFA and WebAuthn for a self-hosted SSH PAM console","description":"How Orion Belt splits console MFA (TOTP, WebAuthn) from SSH public keys and FIDO sk- keys \u2014 configuration pitfalls, rp_id origins, and practical hardening.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["security","ssh","pam"],"keywords":["WebAuthn SSH","MFA privileged access","YubiKey PAM","self-hosted MFA"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Optional SSH certificate authority for self-hosted bastions","permalink":"/blog/optional-ssh-ca"},"nextItem":{"title":"How we designed a 10-minute SSH PAM install path","permalink":"/blog/ten-minute-install-path"}},"content":"Operators will reject MFA that fights SSH muscle memory. Orion Belt splits the problem: **protect the web console** with TOTP and WebAuthn, and keep **SSH on keys** \u2014 including FIDO `sk-*` keys \u2014 that OpenSSH already understands.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nIf you are hardening a **self-hosted privileged access** gateway, this is the MFA model to expect and the footguns to avoid.\\n\\n## Two planes of authentication\\n\\n| Plane | Mechanisms | Goal |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Web console `/ui` | SSH pubkey login, password+TOTP, WebAuthn | Protect admin/approver UX |\\n| SSH to gateway | Registered user keys, optional FIDO SSH keys | Day-to-day access |\\n\\nConfusing these planes creates tickets like \u201Cmy YubiKey works in the browser but not in SSH\u201D \u2014 different protocols, different registration paths.\\n\\n## Console MFA\\n\\n### TOTP\\n\\nFamiliar authenticator-app second factor. Useful when hardware keys are unavailable.\\n\\n### WebAuthn / FIDO2\\n\\nBrowser security keys bound to:\\n\\n- **`rp_id`** \u2014 hostname only (e.g. `orion.example.com` or `localhost`)  \\n- **`origins`** \u2014 full origins the UI is served from (`https://orion.example.com`, maybe a Vite dev origin)\\n\\nIf `rp_id` / origins disagree with the URL in the address bar, registration and login fail in opaque ways. Fix config before blaming the YubiKey.\\n\\nDocker env knobs include `ORION_WEBAUTHN_ENABLED`, `ORION_PUBLIC_HOST`, and `ORION_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` \u2014 see `.env.server.example` in the repo.\\n\\n## SSH keys and FIDO\\n\\nUsers register SSH **public** keys on their Orion identity. OpenSSH then connects as:\\n\\n```bash\\nssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 -p 2222 alice+web-01@gateway\\n```\\n\\nFor hardware-backed SSH keys (`sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com`, etc.), register the public key the same way and touch the device when OpenSSH prompts. Details: main repo `docs/openssh-clients.md`.\\n\\n## Recommended hardening ladder\\n\\n1. Lab: pubkey admin via quickstart  \\n2. Staging: enable TOTP for admins  \\n3. Production: WebAuthn for admins/approvers + network allowlist on UI  \\n4. Require MFA for sensitive console actions as your threat model demands  \\n\\nAlso encrypt session recordings \u2014 MFA does not help if casts sit plaintext on disk ([session recording](/blog/session-recording-is-hard)).\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Can I force MFA on every SSH connection?**  \\nSSH MFA UX varies; many teams prefer strong keys + gateway policy + recorded sessions. Console MFA is the high-ROI control for approvals and replay.\\n\\n**Does WebAuthn replace SSH keys?**  \\nNo. It protects browser sessions to the control plane.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Security guide](/docs/guides/security)  \\n- [Optional SSH CA](/blog/optional-ssh-ca)  \\n- [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes)"},{"id":"ten-minute-install-path","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/ten-minute-install-path","source":"@site/blog/2026-03-12-ten-minute-install-path.md","title":"How we designed a 10-minute SSH PAM install path","description":"Why Orion Belt optimizes for docker-quickstart \u2192 agent dial-out \u2192 recorded SSH. Building a conversion-friendly self-hosted bastion onboarding experience.","date":"2026-03-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"}],"readingTime":1.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ten-minute-install-path","title":"How we designed a 10-minute SSH PAM install path","description":"Why Orion Belt optimizes for docker-quickstart \u2192 agent dial-out \u2192 recorded SSH. Building a conversion-friendly self-hosted bastion onboarding experience.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ops","self-hosted","ssh"],"keywords":["self-hosted SSH bastion","Docker PAM","quickstart","Orion Belt install"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MFA and WebAuthn for a self-hosted SSH PAM console","permalink":"/blog/mfa-webauthn-ssh"},"nextItem":{"title":"Open-source PAM and SSH bastion options compared","permalink":"/blog/open-source-pam-landscape"}},"content":"Most infrastructure products document *everything*. Conversion dies on page one. For Orion Belt, the path we care about is short on purpose:\\n\\n**gateway up \u2192 agent dials out \u2192 SSH works \u2192 session recorded \u2192 wow**\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis post explains why that sequence matters for a **self-hosted SSH bastion / PAM** product, what we automated in `docker-quickstart.sh`, and what we deliberately left for production docs.\\n\\n## The funnel that actually matters\\n\\nStars and HN traffic are useless if the README dumps people into YAML archaeology. Security tools especially need a **trusted first success**: if the demo lies, nobody will harden a real deployment.\\n\\nWe optimize for:\\n\\n1. Clone the repo  \\n2. One script starts Postgres + gateway + admin bootstrap  \\n3. Browser login with an SSH public key  \\n4. **Add agent** \u2192 outbound connect  \\n5. Open a session \u2192 see it under **Sessions**\\n\\nGuide: [Try Orion Belt in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes).\\n\\n## What the quickstart script does\\n\\n`./scripts/docker-quickstart.sh`:\\n\\n- Writes `.env.server` with random Postgres password + JWT secret if missing  \\n- `docker compose up` for the server stack  \\n- Waits for `/health`  \\n- Generates an admin ed25519 keypair if needed  \\n- Runs `setup` to create the first admin  \\n\\nThere is **no** self-service signup form. That is intentional: the control plane is SSH-key identity, not a random password form on the internet.\\n\\n## Why Docker for labs and packages for prod\\n\\n| Audience | Path |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Evaluator / bug repro | Docker quickstart + compose agent |\\n| Homelab lasting a weekend | Docker or packages |\\n| Company gateway | deb/rpm/apk + hardening docs |\\n\\nBoth are real. Collapsing them into one doc produces a bad lab *and* a bad production guide. See [Linux packages and labs](/blog/linux-packages-and-labs).\\n\\n## Friction we refuse to ship in the first ten minutes\\n\\n- Manual SQL  \\n- Hand-editing JWT secrets without a generator  \\n- Requiring a public DNS name before hello-world  \\n- Forcing SSH CA on  \\n\\nThose belong *after* the wow.\\n\\n## Measure the path\\n\\nIf you fork or contribute, break the 10-minute path on purpose and see what fails. Every failure mode should either be fixed or documented in the troubleshooting table \u2014 not discovered on HN launch day.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)  \\n- [Server setup](/docs/quickstart/server)  \\n- [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)"},{"id":"open-source-pam-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/open-source-pam-landscape","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-26-open-source-pam-landscape.md","title":"Open-source PAM and SSH bastion options compared","description":"A practical guide to open-source privileged access and SSH bastions \u2014 Teleport, Boundary, Warpgate, DIY jump hosts, and Orion Belt as a self-hosted SSH-focused PAM gateway.","date":"2026-02-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Teleport","permalink":"/blog/tags/teleport","description":"Comparisons and alternatives"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"},{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"}],"readingTime":2.36,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"open-source-pam-landscape","title":"Open-source PAM and SSH bastion options compared","description":"A practical guide to open-source privileged access and SSH bastions \u2014 Teleport, Boundary, Warpgate, DIY jump hosts, and Orion Belt as a self-hosted SSH-focused PAM gateway.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","teleport","self-hosted","ssh"],"keywords":["open source PAM","Teleport alternative","SSH bastion","self-hosted privileged access","Warpgate","Boundary"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How we designed a 10-minute SSH PAM install path","permalink":"/blog/ten-minute-install-path"},"nextItem":{"title":"Just-in-time (JIT) SSH access with approvals that people will use","permalink":"/blog/jit-access-approvals"}},"content":"Search for **open source PAM** or **Teleport alternative** and you will find overlapping tools that solve different jobs. This guide maps the landscape for SSH-first teams: what each category is good at, where it gets heavy, and where **Orion Belt** fits as a **self-hosted SSH bastion / PAM gateway**.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Categories that get conflated\\n\\n### 1. Jump hosts / classic bastions\\n\\nA hardened box you SSH into, then hop onward. Cheap to start. Weak on recording, JIT, and per-machine policy unless you invent them.\\n\\n### 2. SSH gateways / recording proxies\\n\\nTools that terminate or broker SSH and may record sessions (Warpgate-like products, custom `sshd` ForceCommand setups, etc.). Stronger audit story; feature depth varies.\\n\\n### 3. Identity-centric brokers\\n\\nHashiCorp **Boundary** emphasizes identity and credential brokering in a Hashi ecosystem. Excellent if that is already your center of gravity; different UX if you mainly wanted an SSH bastion.\\n\\n### 4. Broad zero-trust platforms\\n\\n**Teleport** (and similar) cover SSH plus Kubernetes, databases, web apps, Windows, and a large policy surface. Ideal when you need that breadth; heavy when you only needed SSH PAM.\\n\\n### 5. Commercial PAM appliances\\n\\nCyberArk / BeyondTrust class: deep enterprise workflows, price and complexity to match.\\n\\n## Comparison table\\n\\n| Option | Best at | Watch-outs |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| DIY jump host | Tiny labs | No JIT/recording by default |\\n| Warpgate-style gateway | Simple shared SSH gateway | PAM workflows may be thinner |\\n| Boundary | Identity + Hashi stack | Not \u201CSSH bastion UX\u201D first |\\n| Teleport | Multi-protocol zero trust | Ops and learning curve |\\n| Orion Belt | Self-hosted **SSH PAM** slice, reverse agents | Narrower protocol scope in v1 |\\n| Commercial PAM | Enterprise process depth | Cost, lock-in |\\n\\n## Where Orion Belt sits\\n\\nOrion Belt optimizes for:\\n\\n- **Self-hosted privileged access** focused on SSH/SCP  \\n- **Agents dial out** (no inbound SSH required on targets)  \\n- Session **recording + live watch**  \\n- **JIT approvals** (+ ChatOps)  \\n- MFA / WebAuthn, ReBAC, optional SSH CA  \\n- Linux packages + Docker quickstart  \\n\\nIt is intentionally a **lighter Teleport alternative** for teams whose privileged access problem is mostly SSH \u2014 not a claim to replace every Teleport protocol.\\n\\nDeep dive: [Orion Belt vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport). Architecture: [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents).\\n\\n## How to choose in one meeting\\n\\nAsk:\\n\\n1. Do we need **non-SSH protocols** this quarter?  \\n2. Must targets stay **free of inbound SSH**?  \\n3. Is **session recording** a hard requirement?  \\n4. Do we need **JIT approvals** or is standing access OK?  \\n5. Can we run a gateway (Postgres, backups) ourselves?  \\n6. Does license policy allow the **AGPL v3.0**? (Orion Belt moved to it from Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause in 1.5.0.)\\n\\nIf answers skew toward SSH + self-host + recording/JIT, evaluate Orion Belt hands-on:\\n\\n[Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes)\\n\\n## Related reading\\n\\n- [Announcing Orion Belt](/blog/announcing-orion-belt)  \\n- [Bastion vs PAM](/blog/bastion-vs-pam)  \\n- [Orion Belt is now AGPL v3.0](/blog/orion-belt-agpl)"},{"id":"jit-access-approvals","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/jit-access-approvals","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-12-jit-access-approvals.md","title":"Just-in-time (JIT) SSH access with approvals that people will use","description":"How to design JIT privileged access for SSH \u2014 request, approve, expire \u2014 with ChatOps and session recording. Orion Belt\u2019s approach to temporary access workflows.","date":"2026-02-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Ops","permalink":"/blog/tags/ops","description":"Deploying and running Orion Belt"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":2.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"jit-access-approvals","title":"Just-in-time (JIT) SSH access with approvals that people will use","description":"How to design JIT privileged access for SSH \u2014 request, approve, expire \u2014 with ChatOps and session recording. Orion Belt\u2019s approach to temporary access workflows.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","ops","security"],"keywords":["JIT access","temporary privileged access","SSH approval workflow","self-hosted PAM"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Open-source PAM and SSH bastion options compared","permalink":"/blog/open-source-pam-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Relationship-based access control (ReBAC) for SSH machines","permalink":"/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines"}},"content":"Standing SSH privileges are convenient until an ex-contractor key still works on Friday night. **Just-in-time (JIT) access** \u2014 request, approve, expire \u2014 is the standard answer in privileged access management. It only works if the workflow is faster than pasting a key into Slack.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis article covers how JIT fits a **self-hosted SSH PAM** gateway, what UX details matter, and how Orion Belt implements temporary access without making on-call hate you.\\n\\n## Why standing access fails\\n\\n- Keys outlive tickets  \\n- \u201CEmergency\u201D grants become permanent  \\n- Audit asks \u201Cwho had root last month?\u201D and the answer is \u201Ceveryone in `#ops`\u201D  \\n- Compliance frameworks increasingly expect **time-bound** privileged sessions  \\n\\nCommercial PAM vendors built empires on this pain. Open-source and self-hosted teams still under-serve it.\\n\\n## Anatomy of a usable JIT flow\\n\\nA flow people will follow:\\n\\n1. Engineer needs `web-01` for 90 minutes  \\n2. They submit a **reason + duration** (CLI or UI)  \\n3. Approver gets a ping (console bell and/or ChatOps)  \\n4. Approval creates a grant that **auto-expires**  \\n5. Session is **recorded** while they work  \\n6. Grant disappears; recording remains for review  \\n\\nA flow people will bypass:\\n\\n1. Open four tabs of wiki  \\n2. File a ticket in a system nobody watches at 2am  \\n3. Wait for a manager who is asleep  \\n4. \u2026so they reuse yesterday\u2019s shared key  \\n\\n## Orion Belt\u2019s building blocks\\n\\n- **Access requests** with reason and duration  \\n- **Approvals** in the web console  \\n- **ChatOps plugins** for Slack, Discord, Teams, Rocket.Chat \u2014 approvals where the team already lives  \\n- **Recording + live watch** on the gateway path  \\n- ReBAC grants underneath (see [ReBAC for SSH machines](/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines))  \\n\\nExample CLI shape:\\n\\n```bash\\nosh web-01 --request-access --duration 1h --reason \\"Incident 4821 rollback\\"\\n```\\n\\n## ChatOps without turning chat into your IdP\\n\\nChat approvals are great for speed. They are not a substitute for:\\n\\n- Authenticating the approver properly  \\n- Logging who approved what  \\n- Ensuring the bot cannot be spoofed casually  \\n\\nKeep ChatOps as a **notification + action surface** bound back to the gateway\u2019s authz, not a free-for-all emoji vote.\\n\\n## Pair JIT with recording\\n\\nTemporary access without audit just **delays** standing privilege. The conversion story we optimize for in docs is:\\n\\nrequest \u2192 approve \u2192 SSH \u2192 **Sessions playback**\\n\\nLab walkthrough: [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes).\\n\\n## Policy ideas you can copy\\n\\n| Scenario | Duration | Approver | Remote user |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| On-call break-glass | 1\u20132h | Another on-call / lead | root |\\n| Deploy to one app host | 30\u201360m | Service owner | deploy |\\n| Vendor debug | 2\u20134h | Security + owner | restricted user |\\n| Homelab tinkering | 24h | You | whatever you use |\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Does JIT replace MFA?**  \\nNo. MFA protects the console / identity; JIT limits *when* privileged relationships exist.\\n\\n**What if the approver is offline?**  \\nDefine a backup approver rotation. A JIT system with one human bottleneck will be routed around.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Session recording is hard](/blog/session-recording-is-hard)  \\n- [Looking for early operators](/blog/looking-for-early-operators)  \\n- [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ)"},{"id":"rebac-for-ssh-machines","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-28-rebac-for-ssh-machines.md","title":"Relationship-based access control (ReBAC) for SSH machines","description":"How ReBAC maps users to SSH machines and remote users in a self-hosted PAM gateway, when to use OpenFGA, and how Orion Belt keeps grants practical.","date":"2026-01-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture","description":"Design and tradeoffs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":2.51,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"rebac-for-ssh-machines","title":"Relationship-based access control (ReBAC) for SSH machines","description":"How ReBAC maps users to SSH machines and remote users in a self-hosted PAM gateway, when to use OpenFGA, and how Orion Belt keeps grants practical.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["pam","architecture","security"],"keywords":["ReBAC SSH","OpenFGA","privileged access","self-hosted PAM","SSH authorization"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Just-in-time (JIT) SSH access with approvals that people will use","permalink":"/blog/jit-access-approvals"},"nextItem":{"title":"Why SSH session recording is harder than access control","permalink":"/blog/session-recording-is-hard"}},"content":"Most SSH fleets still authorize with a pile of `authorized_keys` files and a spreadsheet nobody updates. **Relationship-based access control (ReBAC)** is a clearer model for a **self-hosted PAM** gateway: users have relationships to machines (and to remote OS users), not a single \u201Cops can root everywhere\u201D bit.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis guide explains how ReBAC shows up in Orion Belt, how it differs from coarse RBAC, and when optional **OpenFGA** is worth introducing.\\n\\n## RBAC vs ReBAC in one paragraph\\n\\n**RBAC** says: Alice has role `sre`, and role `sre` may access production.  \\n**ReBAC** says: Alice is `can_access` on machine `web-01` as remote user `deploy` \u2014 a direct relationship in a graph.\\n\\nRBAC scales via roles; ReBAC scales via relationships. SSH privileged access is naturally relational: *person \u2194 machine \u2194 unix account \u2194 time window*.\\n\\n## How Orion Belt models it\\n\\nAt a practical level:\\n\\n1. **Users** authenticate with SSH public keys (plus optional MFA on the console).  \\n2. **Agents / machines** enroll with the gateway.  \\n3. **Grants** attach a user to a machine with allowed **remote users** (`root`, `ubuntu`, `deploy`, \u2026).  \\n4. JIT requests create **time-bounded** relationships instead of standing grants.\\n\\nUI surfaces: **Users**, **Permissions**, **Agents**. CLI examples live in [User management](/docs/guides/user-management) and the server setup docs.\\n\\n```bash\\norion-belt-server permission grant \\\\\\n  --user alice --machine web-01 --type both --remote-users deploy\\n```\\n\\n## Remote users are still Linux users\\n\\nReBAC does not invent OS accounts. If you grant `alice \u2192 web-01 as root`, the `root` account must exist on the agent host. Orion brokers the session; the agent host still enforces local account reality.\\n\\nThis is intentional. A PAM gateway should not pretend to replace `/etc/passwd`.\\n\\n## Optional OpenFGA\\n\\nSome organizations already run [OpenFGA](https://openfga.dev/) (or similar) as a central authorization service. Orion Belt can optionally delegate authorization checks there instead of only using built-in tables.\\n\\n**Use built-in ReBAC when:**\\n\\n- You want the smallest self-hosted footprint  \\n- Your grant graph is \u201Cusers \u2194 machines\u201D  \\n\\n**Consider OpenFGA when:**\\n\\n- Authorization is already centralized for other apps  \\n- You need richer tuple models shared outside SSH  \\n\\nMisconfiguring external authz is a footgun \u2014 fail closed, and test grants in a lab first.\\n\\n## Least privilege patterns that work\\n\\n1. **Default deny** \u2014 no grant, no SSH through the gateway.  \\n2. **Per-machine grants** instead of \u201Cproduction\u201D mega-roles when possible.  \\n3. **Non-root remote users** for day-to-day work; root via JIT.  \\n4. **Separate auditor identities** for session playback.  \\n5. **Revoke on offboarding** as a checklist item next to GitHub org removal.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Is this Zanzibar?**  \\nReBAC as a family includes Zanzibar-style systems. Orion\u2019s built-in path is the pragmatic SSH subset; OpenFGA is the escape hatch for fuller models.\\n\\n**Can groups map to many machines?**  \\nUse tags/metadata for organization, but remember: **tags are not grants**. Access still needs an explicit permission edge (or a higher-level automation that creates those edges).\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [JIT SSH access](/blog/jit-access-approvals)  \\n- [Machine management](/docs/guides/machine-management)  \\n- [Orion Belt vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport)"},{"id":"session-recording-is-hard","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/session-recording-is-hard","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-09-session-recording-is-hard.md","title":"Why SSH session recording is harder than access control","description":"Building reliable SSH session recording for a self-hosted PAM gateway \u2014 PTY quirks, secret leakage, retention, encryption, and live watch. How Orion Belt approaches auditability.","date":"2026-01-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"}],"readingTime":3.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"session-recording-is-hard","title":"Why SSH session recording is harder than access control","description":"Building reliable SSH session recording for a self-hosted PAM gateway \u2014 PTY quirks, secret leakage, retention, encryption, and live watch. How Orion Belt approaches auditability.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","pam","security"],"keywords":["SSH session recording","privileged access audit","self-hosted PAM","SSH bastion recording"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Relationship-based access control (ReBAC) for SSH machines","permalink":"/blog/rebac-for-ssh-machines"},"nextItem":{"title":"Announcing Orion Belt \u2014 open-source self-hosted SSH PAM / bastion","permalink":"/blog/announcing-orion-belt"}},"content":"Granting \u201Calice may SSH to `web-01`\u201D is the easy half of privileged access. The hard half is answering, months later: **what exactly happened in that shell?** Access control is a graph. **SSH session recording** is a stream of messy terminal I/O, storage policy, and human behavior.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis article is for operators evaluating **self-hosted PAM** or an **SSH bastion** with audit requirements \u2014 SOC-minded startups, regulated teams, and homelabs that still want a paper trail.\\n\\n## Access control vs auditability\\n\\n| Concern | Typical difficulty | Failure mode |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Authn (keys, MFA) | Medium | Stolen key, weak MFA |\\n| Authz (who \u2192 which host) | Medium | Over-broad roles |\\n| **Recording** | High | Gaps, unreadable casts, secret sprawl on disk |\\n| Live watch | High | Latency, privacy, \u201Cwho can watch whom\u201D |\\n\\nMany DIY bastions stop after authz. Then an incident arrives and nobody can replay the session that mattered.\\n\\n## Why terminal recording is messy\\n\\n### PTYs are not logs\\n\\nInteractive SSH is a pseudo-terminal: resize events, ANSI color, cursor motion, binary-ish output from `top` or `vim`. A naive \u201Clog stdout\u201D approach produces files humans cannot replay. Orion Belt uses cast-style recordings suitable for console playback \u2014 the same family of ideas behind asciinema-like players.\\n\\n### Secrets show up in cleartext\\n\\nPeople paste API tokens into shells. Recordings then become **secret stores**. Treat recording storage like a vault:\\n\\n- Encrypt at rest when possible (`recording.encryption_key` in Orion)  \\n- Restrict who can open **Sessions** in the UI  \\n- Set **retention** deliberately  \\n- Train operators not to paste production secrets into recorded sessions (or use short-lived creds)\\n\\n### Completeness depends on the path\\n\\nIf a user SSHs **directly** to the agent host, the gateway never sees the session. Recording is not magic \u2014 it only covers traffic that traverses the gateway. The operational rule has to be cultural and technical: **gateway or it didn\u2019t happen.**\\n\\n## What \u201Cgood\u201D looks like in a PAM gateway\\n\\nFor Orion Belt\u2019s v1 SSH slice we care about:\\n\\n1. **Record by default** on the Docker / recommended path  \\n2. **Playback** in the web console (seek / pause)  \\n3. **Live watch** for active sessions when an admin needs eyes-on  \\n4. **Compression + retention** knobs for disk reality  \\n5. Optional **encryption at rest**\\n\\nWeb terminal sessions are first-class too (`source=web`) \u2014 not only CLI SSH.\\n\\n## Live watch vs recording\\n\\n| | Recording | Live watch |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| When | After / during flush | During active session |\\n| Use | Incident review, compliance | Pairing, suspicious activity |\\n| Risk | Secrets on disk | Privacy / over-surveillance |\\n\\nBoth should be **permissioned**. Silent always-on screen sharing for every admin is how you lose trust inside the company.\\n\\nDeep dive: [Live session watch](/blog/live-session-watch).\\n\\n## Implementation checklist for self-hosted teams\\n\\nBefore you call your **SSH bastion** \u201Cauditable\u201D:\\n\\n1. Force human SSH through the gateway (OpenSSH `user+host@gw` or `osh`)  \\n2. Enable recording; set retention days you can defend  \\n3. Turn on encryption at rest for anything beyond a lab  \\n4. Limit replay roles (auditor vs operator)  \\n5. Test a full loop: connect \u2192 type \u2192 disconnect \u2192 playback  \\n\\nLab path: [Try in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes) ends on the Sessions page for a reason \u2014 that is the conversion moment.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Are recordings a substitute for shell history?**  \\nNo. Histories are per-user, incomplete, and editable. Gateway recordings are a different control.\\n\\n**Can I disable recording?**  \\nYes in config \u2014 not recommended if you claimed PAM-style audit to leadership.\\n\\n**Where are files stored?**  \\nDefault `/var/lib/orion-belt/recordings` (or the Docker volume). Plan disk like you plan log volume.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents)  \\n- [JIT access approvals](/blog/jit-access-approvals)  \\n- [Sessions guide](/docs/guides/sessions)  \\n- [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)"},{"id":"announcing-orion-belt","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/announcing-orion-belt","source":"@site/blog/2025-12-18-announcing-orion-belt.md","title":"Announcing Orion Belt \u2014 open-source self-hosted SSH PAM / bastion","description":"Orion Belt is a self-hosted SSH access gateway with PAM workflows \u2014 reverse agents, session recording, JIT approvals, MFA, and ReBAC. A lighter Teleport alternative for SSH-first teams.","date":"2025-12-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/blog/tags/pam","description":"Privileged access management"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"}],"readingTime":2.7,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"announcing-orion-belt","title":"Announcing Orion Belt \u2014 open-source self-hosted SSH PAM / bastion","description":"Orion Belt is a self-hosted SSH access gateway with PAM workflows \u2014 reverse agents, session recording, JIT approvals, MFA, and ReBAC. A lighter Teleport alternative for SSH-first teams.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","pam","self-hosted"],"keywords":["open source PAM","SSH bastion","self-hosted privileged access","Teleport alternative"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Why SSH session recording is harder than access control","permalink":"/blog/session-recording-is-hard"},"nextItem":{"title":"Why reverse SSH agents beat opening inbound ports on every server","permalink":"/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents"}},"content":"**Orion Belt** is an open-source, **self-hosted SSH access gateway** with PAM-style workflows. It sits in the gap between a raw jump host and a full zero-trust platform: reverse agents, session recording, live watch, JIT approvals, MFA/WebAuthn, ReBAC, and an optional SSH CA \u2014 without SaaS dependency.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThis post is the product introduction for operators evaluating an **open source PAM** option focused on SSH, or a practical **Teleport alternative** when you do not need every protocol on day one.\\n\\n## Who this is for\\n\\n- DevOps / SRE / Linux admins managing fleets over SSH  \\n- Homelab and **self-hosted** users who want audit trails without a vendor cloud  \\n- Small companies that need **privileged access** controls but not a CyberArk deployment  \\n- Teams tired of inbound `:22` on every box \u201Cjust for the bastion\u201D\\n\\n## The problem we kept hitting\\n\\nTraditional SSH access fails in predictable ways:\\n\\n- Long-lived keys copied into chat  \\n- No native approval workflow for break-glass  \\n- Spotty or nonexistent session recording  \\n- Broad VPN access instead of per-machine grants  \\n- \u201CEnterprise PAM\u201D locked behind proprietary licensing and SaaS\\n\\nCommercial products and large open platforms solve real problems \u2014 and often arrive with an ops surface larger than the SSH problem you actually have.\\n\\n## What Orion Belt is (and is not)\\n\\n### It is\\n\\nA **Linux-first SSH/SCP bastion gateway** you run yourself:\\n\\n| Capability | Role |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Reverse agents | Targets dial **out** \u2014 no inbound hole required on the host for Orion |\\n| Gateway | Brokers sessions, enforces ReBAC, records traffic |\\n| JIT access | Request \u2192 approve \u2192 expire (UI / API / ChatOps) |\\n| MFA | TOTP + WebAuthn for the console; FIDO SSH keys supported |\\n| Clients | `osh` / `ocp` / `oadmin`, vanilla OpenSSH, or web terminal |\\n| Packaging | Docker quickstart, deb/rpm/apk |\\n\\n### It is not\\n\\n- A multi-protocol mesh for Kubernetes, Windows RDP, and databases on day one (roadmap items exist; SSH is the v1 promise)  \\n- A hosted SaaS (by design)  \\n- A drop-in replacement for every Teleport feature  \\n\\nPositioning we use deliberately: *Teleport-like SSH access control without opening inbound ports or adopting a large platform.*\\n\\n## Architecture in one diagram\\n\\n```text\\nCLI / OpenSSH / Web UI  \u2192  Gateway (:2222 SSH, :8080 UI)\\n                              \u2191 reverse SSH\\n                           Agent on target\\n```\\n\\nDetails: [Architecture overview](/docs/architecture/overview) and [Why reverse SSH agents](/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents).\\n\\n## License \u2014 said up front\\n\\n:::info[Update, November 2026]\\nSince 1.5.0, Orion Belt is open source under the GNU AGPL v3.0. See\\n[Orion Belt is now AGPL v3.0](/blog/orion-belt-agpl).\\n:::\\n\\nAt launch, Orion Belt shipped under **Apache 2.0 + Commons Clause**:\\n\\n- Free to use, modify, and **self-host**, including internal commercial use  \\n- You **cannot sell** Orion Belt (or a hosted service whose value derives substantially from it) as a product  \\n\\nWe disclose this on community posts (HN, Lobsters, Reddit) because security software requires trust. Full text: [LICENSE](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt/blob/master/LICENSE). More context: [Commons Clause honesty](/blog/commons-clause-honesty).\\n\\n## Try it\\n\\n```bash\\ngit clone https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt.git\\ncd orion-belt\\n./scripts/docker-quickstart.sh\\n```\\n\\nWalkthrough: [Try Orion Belt in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes).\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- Website: [orion-belt.dev](https://orion-belt.dev)  \\n- Source: [github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)  \\n- Chat: [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ)  \\n- Compare: [Orion Belt vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport)"},{"id":"why-reverse-ssh-agents","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/why-reverse-ssh-agents","source":"@site/blog/2025-12-05-why-reverse-ssh-agents.md","title":"Why reverse SSH agents beat opening inbound ports on every server","description":"Learn how reverse SSH tunnels work for bastion and PAM gateways, the security tradeoffs versus inbound SSH, and why Orion Belt agents dial out instead of exposing port 22.","date":"2025-12-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SSH","permalink":"/blog/tags/ssh","description":"SSH access, bastions, and tunnels"},{"inline":false,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture","description":"Design and tradeoffs"},{"inline":false,"label":"Security","permalink":"/blog/tags/security","description":"Threat model and hardening"},{"inline":false,"label":"Self-hosted","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-hosted","description":"Running infrastructure yourself"}],"readingTime":4.6,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Orion Belt","title":"Maintainers","url":"https://orion-belt.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/orion"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/orion-belt-dev.png","key":"orion"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"why-reverse-ssh-agents","title":"Why reverse SSH agents beat opening inbound ports on every server","description":"Learn how reverse SSH tunnels work for bastion and PAM gateways, the security tradeoffs versus inbound SSH, and why Orion Belt agents dial out instead of exposing port 22.","authors":["orion"],"tags":["ssh","architecture","security","self-hosted"],"keywords":["reverse SSH","SSH bastion","reverse tunnel","self-hosted PAM","no inbound SSH"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Announcing Orion Belt \u2014 open-source self-hosted SSH PAM / bastion","permalink":"/blog/announcing-orion-belt"}},"content":"If you run Linux servers at any scale, you already know the uncomfortable default: **someone opens SSH from somewhere**. A bastion host, a VPN, a \u201Ctemporary\u201D security-group rule that outlives the outage. Traditional **SSH bastion** designs still often assume the gateway (or the operator) can reach port 22 on the target.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nOrion Belt takes a different default for its **self-hosted PAM / SSH access gateway**: agents on target machines **dial out** to the gateway over reverse SSH. Operators never need inbound SSH on the application hosts. This article explains why that model exists, how it compares to classic bastions, and which tradeoffs you should accept with eyes open.\\n\\n## The problem with inbound SSH (even \u201Cjust from the bastion\u201D)\\n\\nA classic pattern looks like this:\\n\\n```text\\nOperator \u2192 Bastion \u2192 (SSH :22) \u2192 Target\\n```\\n\\nThat path creates recurring pain:\\n\\n1. **Firewall inventory grows forever** \u2014 every VPC, VLAN, and cloud account needs rules so the bastion can reach targets.\\n2. **Targets stay in the attack surface** \u2014 anything that accepts inbound SSH can be scanned, credential-stuffed, or hit by a compromised jump box.\\n3. **NAT and ephemeral environments fight you** \u2014 laptops, CI runners, and locked-down cloud subnets often cannot be \u201Creached\u201D; they can only dial out.\\n4. **Audit is incomplete** \u2014 if anyone still SSHs direct to the host, your bastion\u2019s session recording never sees it.\\n\\nA **Teleport alternative** or commercial PAM product will solve pieces of this with agents, workers, or IoT tunnels. The underlying idea is the same: **move reachability so the target initiates connectivity**.\\n\\n## What \u201Cagents dial out\u201D means\\n\\nIn Orion Belt:\\n\\n```text\\nOperator \u2192 Gateway (SSH :2222 / HTTPS UI)\\n                \u2191\\n         reverse SSH\\n                \u2191\\n              Agent on target\\n```\\n\\n1. You install a small **agent** on the machine you want to manage.\\n2. The agent authenticates to the **gateway** and keeps a reverse tunnel open (outbound from the target\u2019s perspective).\\n3. When an operator connects to the gateway (CLI, OpenSSH `user+machine@gateway`, or web terminal), the gateway **brokers** the session through that tunnel.\\n4. Policy (ReBAC), MFA on the console, JIT approvals, and **session recording / live watch** all sit on the gateway path.\\n\\nThe target still runs Linux users and SSH semantics for the remote account you land on (`root`, `ubuntu`, \u2026). What changes is **who is allowed to open a connection toward that host from the network**.\\n\\n## Security tradeoffs (honest)\\n\\n### Gains\\n\\n- **No inbound SSH required on targets** for the Orion path \u2014 useful for hardened subnets and \u201Cdeny all ingress\u201D baselines.\\n- **One control plane** for authentication, authorization, and recording.\\n- **Works behind NAT** as long as the agent can reach the gateway on the SSH port (typically **2222**).\\n\\n### Costs\\n\\n- **Offline agent = unreachable host** until it reconnects. There is no magic wake-up if the process is dead.\\n- **The gateway becomes high-value infrastructure.** Treat it like a domain controller for SSH: patch it, restrict who can reach `:2222` / the UI, encrypt recordings, require MFA for admins.\\n- **You must trust and update the agent binary** on every enrolled machine \u2014 same class of risk as any other node agent (Teleport, monitoring, backup).\\n\\nIf your environment cannot allow **outbound** connections from targets to a central gateway, reverse agents are the wrong tool. Pick a model that matches your network policy.\\n\\n## Reverse tunnels vs VPN vs classic bastion\\n\\n| Approach | Typical exposure | Recording / JIT | Ops weight |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Direct SSH to hosts | Inbound :22 everywhere | DIY | Low features |\\n| Classic bastion | Inbound to bastion + often bastion\u2192host :22 | Rare / custom | Low\u2013medium |\\n| Full-mesh VPN | Broad L3 reachability | Separate tools | Medium |\\n| Reverse-agent gateway (Orion Belt) | Inbound mainly to gateway; agents outbound | Built-in path | Medium, SSH-focused |\\n\\nVPNs solve \u201Chow do packets route.\u201D They do not automatically give you **privileged access management**: approvals, per-machine grants, or session playback. A **self-hosted privileged access** gateway sits *above* connectivity.\\n\\n## When reverse agents are the right default\\n\\nChoose this model if most of these are true:\\n\\n- Your privileged access problem is **mostly SSH**\\n- You want a **self-hosted SSH bastion** without SaaS lock-in\\n- Security wants **no inbound SSH** on app tiers\\n- You need **recording + JIT** without buying a CyberArk-class appliance\\n- You are okay operating a gateway (Postgres, backups, hardening)\\n\\nPrefer a broader platform (for example Teleport) if you need first-class Kubernetes, database, Windows, and web app access in one product on day one. See our comparison: [Orion Belt vs Teleport](/blog/orion-belt-vs-teleport).\\n\\n## Try the model in about ten minutes\\n\\nThe fastest way to feel the tradeoff is not a whitepaper:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit clone https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt.git\\ncd orion-belt\\n./scripts/docker-quickstart.sh\\n```\\n\\nThen follow [Try Orion Belt in 10 minutes](/docs/quickstart/try-in-10-minutes): register an agent, dial out, SSH through the gateway, open **Sessions** for playback.\\n\\n## FAQ\\n\\n**Is reverse SSH the same as `ssh -R`?**  \\nConceptually related: the remote side opens a channel back toward you. Orion Belt productizes that idea with enrollment, policy, and recording \u2014 not a one-off shell tunnel.\\n\\n**Do I still expose port 22 on the target?**  \\nNot for the Orion path. You may leave local SSH for break-glass; just know those sessions bypass gateway recording.\\n\\n**What about lateral movement from a compromised gateway?**  \\nA compromised gateway is bad in *any* bastion design. Mitigate with MFA, least privilege, network allowlists, recording encryption, and host hardening \u2014 see the main repo\u2019s deployment hardening docs.\\n\\n## Next reading\\n\\n- [Announcing Orion Belt](/blog/announcing-orion-belt)\\n- [Session recording is hard](/blog/session-recording-is-hard)\\n- [Architecture overview](/docs/architecture/overview)\\n- [Discord](https://discord.gg/w62S8jxTHJ) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/orion-belt-dev/orion-belt)"}]}}')}}]);